var e={"cardiovascular-hypertension":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and classification`,points:[`Hypertension is a confirmed office BP of **140/90 mmHg or more**.`,`Confirm with home BP **135/85 or more** or 24-hour ambulatory BP **130/80 or more**.`,`One visit is enough if BP is **180/110 or more** or organ damage is present.`,`ESC 2024 uses three categories: **non-elevated**, **elevated** and **hypertension**.`,`**White-coat hypertension**: high office BP but normal home BP; lifestyle and yearly review.`,`**Resistant hypertension**: BP above target despite three drugs, including a diuretic, at full doses.`,`**Emergency** means severe BP with acute organ damage; without damage it is severe hypertension.`]},{title:`Causes and risk factors`,points:[`Over 90% is **essential hypertension**, driven by salt, obesity, inactivity, age and genes.`,`South Asians are often **salt sensitive**, so high salt intake raises their BP more.`,`Commonest secondary causes are **CKD** and **primary aldosteronism**.`,`Other secondary causes: renal artery stenosis, sleep apnoea, phaeochromocytoma, Cushing syndrome, coarctation.`,`Drugs that raise BP: **NSAIDs**, oral contraceptives, steroids, decongestants and herbal remedies.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Salt retention resets **pressure natriuresis**, so the kidney needs a higher BP to excrete salt.`,`2. **Angiotensin II** constricts arterioles and releases aldosterone, which retains sodium.`,`3. Sympathetic overactivity and endothelial dysfunction add vasoconstriction.`,`4. Small arteries remodel, fixing **peripheral resistance** at a higher level.`,`5. The heart develops **concentric LVH** and diastolic dysfunction.`,`6. Organ damage follows: stroke, MI, heart failure, CKD and retinopathy.`,`7. Autoregulation resets upwards, so a rapid fall in BP causes ischaemia.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Most patients have **no symptoms**; headache is not a guide to BP.`,`Ask about organ symptoms: TIA, angina, breathlessness, claudication and frothy urine.`,`Take a full **drug history**, including NSAIDs, oral pills and ayurvedic remedies.`,`Ask about salt in the patient's words: pickle, papad, namkeen and packaged snacks.`,`Ask about **snoring** and daytime sleepiness, which suggest sleep apnoea.`,`Ask about paroxysms of headache, sweating and palpitations, which suggest phaeochromocytoma.`,`In women ask about **pregnancy plans**, because ACE inhibitors and ARBs are teratogenic.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Seat the patient for 5 minutes and use a cuff covering 80% of the arm.`,`Take **three readings and average the last two**; check both arms at the first visit.`,`Check standing BP in the elderly; a fall of 20/10 is **orthostatic hypotension**.`,`Measure BMI and waist with Indian cut-offs: BMI 23 and waist 90/80 cm.`,`Feel radial and femoral pulses together for **radio-femoral delay** (coarctation).`,`Do **fundoscopy**; flame haemorrhages or papilloedema mean malignant hypertension.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Urine albumin-creatinine ratio**: 30 mg/g or more is the earliest organ damage.`,`Creatinine, eGFR, sodium and potassium are needed before starting RAS blockers.`,`HbA1c and lipids help estimate total cardiovascular risk.`,`ECG for LVH: **Sokolow-Lyon over 35 mm** reclassifies the patient as high risk.`,`Screen for **primary aldosteronism** with aldosterone-renin ratio in all resistant hypertension.`,`Use the **WHO/ISH South-East Asia chart** to estimate 10-year cardiovascular risk.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Advise salt **under 5 g a day**, DASH-style Indian diet and 150 minutes of weekly exercise.`,`Each kilogram of weight lost lowers BP by about 1 mmHg.`,`Start most patients on a **low-dose two-drug single pill**.`,`The pill combines a RAS blocker with a CCB or thiazide-like diuretic.`,`IHCI step 1 is **amlodipine 5 mg**, then add telmisartan 40 mg.`,`Target systolic is **120-129 mmHg** if tolerated; individualise in the frail and over 85.`,`Titrate every **2-4 weeks** and aim for control within 3 months.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Step up to amlodipine 10 mg plus telmisartan 80 mg.`,`Then add **chlorthalidone 12.5-25 mg** to make triple therapy.`,`Before adding any drug, check adherence, cuff technique, salt, NSAIDs and alcohol.`,`The fourth drug for resistant hypertension is **spironolactone 25 mg**.`,`If spironolactone is not tolerated, use eplerenone, amiloride, a beta-blocker or an alpha-blocker.`,`Never combine an **ACE inhibitor with an ARB**.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`**Pregnancy**: use labetalol, nifedipine extended-release or methyldopa.`,`Stop ACE inhibitors, ARBs and spironolactone as soon as pregnancy is planned or confirmed.`,`Diabetes or CKD with albuminuria: use an ACE inhibitor or ARB, plus SGLT2 inhibitor in CKD.`,`Elderly with isolated systolic hypertension: a CCB or diuretic works best.`,`Children with hypertension need a search for **renal causes and coarctation** first.`]},{title:`Red flags, referral and follow-up`,points:[`Emergency: lower mean arterial pressure by **no more than 20-25%** in the first hour.`,`First-line emergency drug is IV labetalol, **20 mg then 20-80 mg every 10 minutes**.`,`Aortic dissection is the exception: systolic under 120 within 20 minutes.`,`**Never give sublingual nifedipine**; the uncontrolled fall causes stroke and MI.`,`Check creatinine and potassium **1-2 weeks** after starting a RAS blocker or diuretic.`,`A creatinine rise up to **30%** is expected; stop the drug if it rises more.`,`Refer suspected secondary hypertension: onset under 40, hypokalaemia, renal bruit or paroxysms.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for hypertension`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Amlodipine (CCB)`,`2.5-10 mg oral once daily`,`Lifelong`,`Ankle oedema (not helped by diuretic), flushing, gum hypertrophy`],[`Telmisartan (ARB)`,`20-80 mg oral once daily (usual 40)`,`Lifelong`,`Hyperkalaemia, creatinine rise; teratogenic`],[`Ramipril (ACE inhibitor)`,`2.5 mg oral daily, up to 10 mg`,`Lifelong`,`Dry cough, angioedema, hyperkalaemia; teratogenic`],[`Chlorthalidone`,`12.5-25 mg oral every morning`,`Lifelong`,`Low potassium and sodium, gout, high glucose`],[`Indapamide SR`,`1.5 mg oral every morning`,`Lifelong`,`Hyponatraemia, hypokalaemia`],[`Spironolactone`,`25 mg oral daily, up to 50 mg`,`Resistant hypertension, long term`,`Hyperkalaemia, gynaecomastia; check K at 1-2 weeks`],[`Metoprolol succinate`,`25-200 mg oral once daily`,`Compelling indication only`,`Bradycardia, fatigue; never stop abruptly`],[`Labetalol`,`Oral 100 mg twice daily; IV 20 mg then 20-80 mg`,`Pregnancy; IV in emergency`,`Postural drop, bronchospasm; IV max 300 mg`],[`Nifedipine extended-release`,`20-30 mg oral once or twice daily`,`Pregnancy, up to 120 mg/day`,`Headache, flushing; never sublingual`]]},{heading:`ESC 2024 BP categories`,columns:[`Category`,`Office BP (mmHg)`,`Action`],rows:[[`Non-elevated`,`Under 120/70`,`Lifestyle; recheck within 3 years`],[`Elevated`,`120-139 / 70-89`,`Lifestyle; drugs if 130/80+ and high risk`],[`Hypertension`,`140/90 or more`,`Lifestyle plus two-drug single pill`],[`Emergency`,`Usually 180/110 with organ damage`,`Admit, IV drugs, controlled lowering`]]},{heading:`Emergency: target by situation`,columns:[`Situation`,`Target`,`Drug`],rows:[[`Malignant HT or encephalopathy`,`MAP down 20-25% in 1 hour`,`IV labetalol or nicardipine`],[`Aortic dissection`,`Systolic under 120, HR under 60 in 20 min`,`IV esmolol or labetalol`],[`Pulmonary oedema or ACS`,`Systolic under 140`,`IV nitroglycerin plus loop diuretic`],[`Ischaemic stroke`,`Treat only over 220/120; 185/110 before lysis`,`IV labetalol`],[`Severe pre-eclampsia`,`Under 160/110 within 1 hour`,`Nifedipine or labetalol plus MgSO4`]]}],remember:[`India diagnoses at 140/90; home 135/85; ABPM 130/80.`,`Start with two drugs in one pill, not one drug titrated slowly.`,`Fourth drug = spironolactone 25 mg, after excluding pseudo-resistance.`,`Amlodipine oedema: halve the dose or add ACEi/ARB, not a diuretic.`,`ACEi cough: switch to ARB, never cough syrup.`,`Creatinine rise up to 30% on ACEi/ARB is expected; continue.`,`Emergency: no more than 25% fall in the first hour, except dissection.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`AHA/ACC 2025: US guideline keeps 130/80 for stage 1, target under 130/80 for all adults, PREVENT risk calculator - India still diagnoses at 140/90.`,`AHA/ACC 2025: 'hypertensive urgency' renamed severe hypertension (over 180/120) without acute organ damage.`,`AHA/ACC 2025: screen every resistant hypertensive for primary aldosteronism whatever the potassium.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Hypertension`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Confirmed office BP **140/90 or more** (home **135/85**, 24-h ABPM **130/80**) on 2-3 visits; one visit enough if **180/110 or more** or organ damage.`],[`Relevant classification`,`ESC 2024: **non-elevated under 120/70**, **elevated 120-139/70-89**, **hypertension 140/90 or more**; white-coat, masked, resistant (3 drugs incl. diuretic), emergency vs severe hypertension without organ damage.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Essential in over 90%; salt, obesity, alcohol, inactivity, age, family history; secondary - CKD, **primary aldosteronism**, renal artery stenosis, OSA, phaeo, Cushing, coarctation, drugs (NSAIDs, OCP, steroids).`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Salt + RAAS + sympathetic drive -> raised peripheral resistance -> vascular remodelling, LVH -> **stroke, MI, HF, CKD, retinopathy**; reset autoregulation -> never drop over 25% in first hour.`],[`Key history`,`Duration, past readings, organ symptoms (TIA, angina, orthopnoea), **drug history (NSAIDs, OCP, herbal)**, salt (pickle, papad), tobacco, alcohol, snoring, paroxysms, pregnancy plans, adherence barriers.`],[`Key examination`,`Correct technique: seated 5 min, right cuff, **3 readings average last 2**, both arms; standing BP in elderly; BMI/waist (23/25, 90/80 cm); radio-femoral delay; bruits; **fundoscopy**.`],[`Investigations`,`**UACR** (30 mg/g or more = damage), creatinine/eGFR, Na/K (K over 5.5 - no ACEi), HbA1c, lipids, **ECG for LVH (Sokolow-Lyon over 35 mm)**, uric acid, TSH; aldosterone-renin ratio when indicated.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Office 140/90 confirmed by home 135/85 or ABPM 130/80; **WHO/ISH SEA chart** for 10-year risk decides drugs in elevated BP (10% or more).`],[`Treatment - lifestyle`,`**Salt under 5 g/day**, K-enriched salt if kidneys normal, DASH Indian plate, **150 min/week exercise**, weight (1 mmHg per kg), stop tobacco, minimal alcohol, home BP monitoring, NP-NCD/IHCI enrolment.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Low-dose two-drug single pill**: RAS blocker + CCB or thiazide-like (telmisartan 40 + amlodipine 5 mg); IHCI: **amlodipine 5 -> +telmisartan 40 -> 10/80 -> +chlorthalidone 12.5-25 mg**, titrate every 2-4 weeks.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Triple single pill (RAS + CCB + thiazide-like) -> resistant: **spironolactone 25 mg** (to 50), else eplerenone, amiloride, beta-blocker, alpha-blocker; renal denervation at expert centre.`],[`Treatment targets and how to use`,`**Systolic 120-129 if tolerated** (ESC); individualise if 85+, frail, orthostatic; once daily at a time remembered (TIME trial); check creatinine/K at 1-2 weeks after RAS blocker - accept 30% rise.`],[`Special groups`,`**Pregnancy**: labetalol, nifedipine ER, methyldopa - no ACEi/ARB; diabetes/CKD with albuminuria: ACEi/ARB + SGLT2i; elderly ISH: CCB or diuretic; child: exclude renal causes and coarctation.`],[`Emergency`,`MAP down **max 20-25% in first hour**, then 160/100 over 2-6 h; **IV labetalol 20 mg then 20-80 mg q10 min (max 300 mg)**; dissection: SBP under 120 in 20 min (esmolol); **never sublingual nifedipine**.`],[`Complications`,`Stroke (commonest in India), ICH, MI, HFpEF, AF, CKD, aortic dissection, retinopathy, vascular dementia, malignant hypertension.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`BP 180/110 with organ symptoms, papilloedema/flame haemorrhages, **pregnancy 160/110**, hypokalaemia or onset under 40, creatinine rise over 30% or K over 5.5 on RAS blocker, child or radio-femoral delay.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Every **2-4 weeks while titrating**, control within 3 months, then 3-6 monthly; creatinine/K yearly; UACR, lipids, HbA1c, ECG yearly; check adherence before adding a drug.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**NP-NCD population screening at 30+**, IHCI protocol-based free drugs, India Hypertension Control Initiative treatment cards; salt reduction (Eat Right India).`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**AHA/ACC 2025**: target under 130/80, PREVENT, 'urgency' becomes severe hypertension, screen all resistant HT for aldosteronism; ESC 2024 three categories still guide India.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`ACEi cough - switch to ARB; amlodipine oedema - not a diuretic problem; never ACEi + ARB; 4th drug = spironolactone; headache is not a guide to BP.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Raised BP in the OPD: from reading to decision`,steps:[{label:`Office BP measured correctly`,detail:`Seated 5 min, 3 readings, average last 2, both arms`},{label:`180/110 or more?`,detail:`Look for acute organ damage: chest pain, deficit, papilloedema`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Organ damage present - emergency`,detail:`Admit, IV labetalol, MAP down max 25% in 1 h`,tone:`warn`},{label:`140-179/90-109 - confirm out of office`,detail:`Home 135/85 or ABPM 130/80`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Elevated 120-139/70-89 - lifestyle first`,detail:`Drugs at 3 months if 130/80+ and 10-y risk 10%+, DM, CKD, CVD`},{label:`Assess risk and organ damage`,detail:`UACR, creatinine, K, HbA1c, lipids, ECG, fundus`},{label:`Start lifestyle + two-drug single pill`,detail:`Telmisartan 40 + amlodipine 5 mg; review 2-4 weeks`},{label:`Controlled: systolic 120-129`,detail:`Review 3-6 monthly, labs yearly`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Drug escalation ladder (ESC 2024 / IHCI)`,steps:[{label:`Step 1: two-drug low-dose single pill`,detail:`ARB/ACEi + CCB or thiazide-like; IHCI amlodipine 5 then add telmisartan 40`},{label:`Step 2: full-dose dual combination`,detail:`Amlodipine 10 + telmisartan 80 mg`},{label:`Step 3: triple therapy`,detail:`Add chlorthalidone 12.5-25 or indapamide SR 1.5 mg`},{label:`Step 4: resistant - add spironolactone`,detail:`25 mg (to 50) if K under 4.5-5.0 and eGFR over 45; check K at 1-2 and 4 weeks`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Step 5: alternatives and specialist`,detail:`Eplerenone, amiloride, beta-blocker, alpha-blocker, clonidine; renal denervation`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Hypertensive emergency: target and drug by organ`,columns:[`Situation`,`Target`,`Drug`],rows:[[`Malignant HT / encephalopathy`,`MAP down 20-25% in 1 h`,`IV labetalol or nicardipine`],[`Aortic dissection`,`SBP under 120, HR under 60 in 20 min`,`IV esmolol/labetalol, then vasodilator`],[`Pulmonary oedema / ACS`,`SBP under 140`,`IV nitroglycerin + loop diuretic`],[`Ischaemic stroke`,`Treat only over 220/120; under 185/110 before lysis`,`IV labetalol`],[`Severe pre-eclampsia`,`Under 160/110 within 1 h`,`Oral nifedipine, IV labetalol or hydralazine + MgSO4`]]}]},"cardiovascular-stable-angina":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Chronic coronary syndrome** is coronary artery disease outside an acute coronary syndrome.`,`Angina is chest discomfort caused by **myocardial ischaemia**.`,`Classic angina is retrosternal, brought on by **exertion or emotion**.`,`It is relieved by **rest or nitrate within 5 minutes**.`,`Stable means predictable: same threshold and same duration every time.`,`Indians get coronary disease **about 10 years earlier**, often with diffuse multivessel disease.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Typical angina** has all three classic features; atypical has two.`,`Non-anginal chest pain has one feature or none.`,`Severity is graded by the **Canadian Cardiovascular Society** classes I to IV.`,`ESC 2024 groups **clinical likelihood** of obstructive CAD from very low to very high.`,`New, rest, nocturnal or crescendo angina is **unstable angina**, which is an ACS.`,`Vasospastic (Prinzmetal) angina causes rest pain at night with transient ST elevation.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. LDL enters a damaged intima and becomes **oxidised**.`,`2. Monocytes enter, eat the LDL and become **foam cells**.`,`3. Smooth muscle cells build a **fibrous cap** over a lipid core.`,`4. A stenosis over **70%** limits the rise in flow on exertion.`,`5. Demand rises with heart rate, contractility and wall tension, so ischaemia appears.`,`6. The **subendocardium** suffers first, so stable ischaemia shows as ST depression.`,`7. A thin-capped vulnerable plaque can rupture elsewhere and cause ACS.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask site, character, radiation, **duration 2-10 minutes**, trigger and relief.`,`Grade the exercise threshold in plain terms to assign the CCS class.`,`Ask red-flag questions: pain at rest, at night, over 20 minutes or new.`,`List risk factors, including **premature CAD** in a first-degree relative.`,`Ask about **PDE-5 inhibitors** (sildenafil, tadalafil), because nitrates are then contraindicated.`,`In a known patient, weekly sublingual nitrate use is the honest measure of control.`,`Elderly, women and diabetics often present with breathlessness or fatigue instead of pain.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Examination is **often normal**; its job is to find risk factors and mimics.`,`Record BP, pulse rate, BMI and waist; these are titration targets.`,`Look for **pallor**, thyroid signs and xanthomas.`,`An ejection systolic murmur with slow-rising pulse suggests **aortic stenosis**; avoid nitrates.`,`Look for heart failure signs and peripheral bruits.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Resting ECG** in everyone; it is normal in over half of patients.`,`Bloods: haemoglobin, fasting lipids, HbA1c, creatinine and TSH if suggested.`,`**Echocardiography** for LV function, wall motion and valve disease.`,`Low to moderate likelihood: **CT coronary angiography** is the preferred first test.`,`Moderate to high likelihood: functional imaging such as stress echo or SPECT.`,`Treadmill test is positive with **1 mm horizontal or downsloping ST depression**.`,`Very high likelihood or high-risk features: invasive coronary angiography.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Think in two parts: drugs that **prevent events** and drugs that **relieve angina**.`,`Give **aspirin 75-100 mg** daily lifelong; clopidogrel 75 mg if aspirin is not tolerated.`,`Give a high-intensity statin to reach **LDL under 55 mg/dL** and a 50% reduction.`,`First anti-anginal is a **beta-blocker and/or CCB**, titrated to heart rate 55-60.`,`Give sublingual nitroglycerin for attacks and before known triggers.`,`Stop tobacco in every form; offer cardiac rehabilitation and influenza vaccine.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`If angina persists, add amlodipine to the beta-blocker, or a long-acting nitrate.`,`Second-line add-ons are **ranolazine**, trimetazidine, nicorandil and ivabradine.`,`Add **colchicine 0.5 mg** daily to reduce events on top of statins.`,`If LDL is above target, add ezetimibe, then a PCSK9 inhibitor or inclisiran.`,`Add an ACE inhibitor or ARB with hypertension, diabetes, CKD or LVEF 40% or less.`,`Revascularisation is for refractory angina or high-risk anatomy such as **left main disease**.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`In diabetes, add an **SGLT2 inhibitor or GLP-1 agonist** with proven benefit.`,`CABG is preferred in diabetics with multivessel disease.`,`Vasospastic angina: CCB plus long-acting nitrate; avoid non-selective beta-blockers.`,`After an elective stent: aspirin plus clopidogrel for **6 months**, then one antiplatelet.`]},{title:`Red flags, referral and follow-up`,points:[`Pain at rest, over 20 minutes or not relieved by 3 nitrates means **ACS**; send by ambulance.`,`New angina within 2 months or crescendo angina needs same-day referral.`,`Exertional syncope with an aortic murmur suggests severe aortic stenosis.`,`Never give nitrates within 24-48 hours of a **PDE-5 inhibitor**.`,`Review 2-4 weeks after any drug change, then every 3-6 months.`,`Check lipids **4-8 weeks** after starting a statin, then yearly.`,`Refer routinely for CCS III-IV despite two anti-anginals, or LVEF under 50%.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for stable angina`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Aspirin`,`75-100 mg oral once daily`,`Lifelong`,`Dyspepsia, GI bleed; add PPI if high risk`],[`Clopidogrel`,`75 mg oral once daily`,`Lifelong if aspirin intolerant`,`Bleeding; avoid omeprazole`],[`Atorvastatin`,`40-80 mg oral once daily`,`Lifelong`,`Myalgia, raised transaminases`],[`Metoprolol succinate`,`25-200 mg oral once daily`,`Long term, rate 55-60`,`Bradycardia, fatigue; never stop suddenly`],[`Diltiazem`,`60 mg three times daily or SR 90-180 mg twice daily`,`Long term`,`Heart block; not with beta-blocker or LVEF under 40%`],[`Amlodipine`,`5-10 mg oral once daily`,`Long term`,`Ankle oedema; safe with beta-blocker`],[`Nitroglycerin`,`0.4-0.5 mg sublingual, repeat every 5 min, max 3`,`For attacks`,`Headache, hypotension; not after PDE-5 inhibitor`],[`Isosorbide mononitrate SR`,`30-60 mg oral every morning`,`Long term`,`Needs 8-12 h nitrate-free interval`],[`Ranolazine`,`375-500 mg twice daily, up to 1000 mg`,`Long term`,`QT prolongation; avoid strong CYP3A4 inhibitors`],[`Colchicine`,`0.5 mg oral once daily`,`Long term`,`Diarrhoea; avoid eGFR under 30`]]},{heading:`Canadian Cardiovascular Society grading`,columns:[`Class`,`Angina comes on with`,`Example`],rows:[[`I`,`Strenuous or prolonged exertion only`,`Running for a bus`],[`II`,`Slight limitation of ordinary activity`,`Brisk stairs, uphill, after meals`],[`III`,`Marked limitation of ordinary activity`,`1-2 blocks on level ground`],[`IV`,`Any activity, or at rest`,`Dressing or bathing; reassess as ACS`]]},{heading:`ESC 2024 likelihood and first test`,columns:[`Likelihood of obstructive CAD`,`First test`],rows:[[`Very low (5% or less)`,`No test; look for another cause`],[`Low (over 5-15%)`,`CT coronary angiography`],[`Moderate (over 15-50%)`,`CT angiography or functional imaging`],[`High (over 50-85%)`,`Functional imaging; TMT if imaging unavailable`],[`Very high (over 85%)`,`Invasive coronary angiography`]]}],remember:[`Medicines prevent death; stents relieve symptoms in stable disease (ISCHEMIA trial).`,`A normal resting ECG does not exclude angina.`,`Titrate the beta-blocker to heart rate 55-60, not to a fixed dose.`,`Every long-acting nitrate needs an 8-12 hour nitrate-free interval.`,`Never combine verapamil or diltiazem with a beta-blocker; amlodipine is the safe partner.`,`Ivabradine works only in sinus rhythm.`,`LDL target under 55: statin, then ezetimibe, then PCSK9 inhibitor.`]},checked:`2026-10`,master:{heading:`Master revision table - Chronic coronary syndrome`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Chronic coronary syndrome**: CAD outside an ACS - stable angina, ANOCA/INOCA, vasospasm, stable post-ACS; angina = **exertional retrosternal constriction relieved by rest or nitrate within 5 minutes**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`Typical (3 features), atypical (2), non-anginal (0-1); **CCS class I-IV**; ESC 2024 clinical likelihood very low 5% or less, low over 5-15, moderate 15-50, high 50-85, very high over 85%.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Atherosclerosis (Indians ~10 years earlier, diffuse disease, diabetes, high Lp(a)); tobacco, HT, DM, dyslipidaemia, CKD, family history; mimics - AS, HCM, anaemia, thyrotoxicosis.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Endothelial dysfunction -> LDL retention/oxidation -> foam cells -> fibroatheroma -> **over 70% stenosis** -> supply-demand mismatch on exertion -> **subendocardial ischaemia (ST depression)**.`],[`Key history`,`Site, character, duration 2-10 min, trigger, relief; CCS grade; **red flags of ACS** (rest, new, crescendo, over 20 min); PDE-5 inhibitor use; risk factors; weekly nitrate count.`],[`Key examination`,`Often normal; BP both arms, pulse, BMI; pallor, thyroid, **xanthomas/arcus under 45**; **ejection systolic murmur (AS)**, HCM murmur; HF signs; bruits, peripheral pulses.`],[`Investigations`,`Resting ECG, Hb, lipids (+Lp(a) once), HbA1c, creatinine, TSH; **echo for LVEF/valves**; test by likelihood - **CCTA (low-moderate)**, functional imaging (moderate-high), ICA (very high).`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Clinical likelihood + test: **CCTA normal rules out**; TMT positive = **1 mm or more horizontal/downsloping ST depression 80 ms after J point**; TMT unreadable with LBBB, paced, WPW, digoxin.`],[`Treatment - event prevention`,`**Aspirin 75-100 mg** (or clopidogrel 75 mg), **atorvastatin 40-80 / rosuvastatin 20-40 mg to LDL under 55 mg/dL and 50% fall**, ACEi/ARB if HT/DM/CKD/LVEF 40% or less, **colchicine 0.5 mg**, SGLT2i/GLP-1 RA in DM.`],[`Treatment - first line (anti-anginal)`,`**Beta-blocker** (metoprolol succinate 25-200 mg, bisoprolol 2.5-10 mg) and/or **CCB** - target resting HR **55-60**; **SL nitroglycerin 0.4-0.5 mg** every 5 min x 3 for attacks.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Add amlodipine to beta-blocker; **ISMN SR 30-60 mg morning (8-12 h nitrate-free)**, ranolazine, trimetazidine MR 35 mg BD, nicorandil, ivabradine (sinus rhythm; not if LVEF over 40% without HF); ezetimibe -> PCSK9i for LDL.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`**Revascularisation** for refractory angina, or prognosis in **left main, 3-vessel/proximal LAD with LVEF 35% or less**; CABG in diabetic multivessel; after elective stent **DAPT 6 months**.`],[`Special groups`,`Vasospasm: CCB + nitrate, avoid non-selective beta-blockers; women/diabetics/elderly - anginal equivalents; asthma - rate-limiting CCB; never verapamil/diltiazem with a beta-blocker.`],[`Complications`,`ACS, sudden death, ischaemic cardiomyopathy and HF, arrhythmia, stent thrombosis if DAPT stopped early.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Rest/new/crescendo angina, pain over 20 min or not relieved by 3 nitrates, syncope, HF, dynamic ST change - **same day**; CCS III-IV despite 2 drugs - routine referral.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`2-4 weeks after drug change, then 3-6 monthly year 1; **lipids 4-8 weeks**, then yearly; targets LDL under 55, BP under 130/80, HbA1c under 7%, HR 55-60, no tobacco.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Tobacco cessation, cardiac rehab, Indian heart-healthy diet, BMI under 23, influenza vaccine, treat depression and OSA; semaglutide in obesity (SELECT).`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**ESC 2024 CCS**: risk factor-weighted clinical likelihood (very low 5% or less - no test), **CCTA first in low-moderate**, functional imaging over TMT, colchicine 0.5 mg (IIa), no ivabradine add-on if LVEF over 40% without HF.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Medicines prevent death, stents relieve symptoms (ISCHEMIA); ivabradine useless in AF; nitrate-free interval mandatory; no nitrate within 24 h of sildenafil, 48 h of tadalafil.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Suspected angina: likelihood decides the first test`,steps:[{label:`Chest pain - any ACS red flag?`,detail:`Rest, new, crescendo, over 20 min, dynamic ECG`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Yes - treat as ACS, ambulance`,tone:`warn`},{label:`No - ECG, bloods, echo`,detail:`LVEF, AS, Hb, lipids, HbA1c, TSH`},{label:`Estimate clinical likelihood (ESC 2024)`,detail:`Symptoms, age, sex, risk factors`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Very low 5% or less - look elsewhere`},{label:`Low-moderate - CT coronary angiography`,detail:`Normal CCTA rules out obstructive CAD`},{label:`Moderate-high - functional imaging; very high - ICA`,detail:`TMT only if imaging unavailable`},{label:`Diagnosis made - start OMT`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Optimal medical therapy in CCS`,steps:[{label:`Everyone: event-prevention drugs`,detail:`Aspirin 75-100 mg, high-intensity statin to LDL under 55, ACEi/ARB if indicated`},{label:`Step 1: beta-blocker and/or CCB`,detail:`Metoprolol succinate 25-200 mg or bisoprolol 2.5-10 mg; HR 55-60`},{label:`Step 2: combine beta-blocker + amlodipine 5-10 mg`,detail:`Never verapamil/diltiazem with a beta-blocker`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Step 3: add long-acting nitrate or second-line`,detail:`ISMN SR 30-60 mg AM; ranolazine 375-750 mg BD; trimetazidine 35 mg BD; nicorandil`},{label:`Step 4: refractory - revascularise`,detail:`PCI or CABG; prognostic in left main / 3-vessel with LVEF 35% or less`,tone:`good`}]}]},"cardiovascular-acs":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Acute coronary syndrome** is acute myocardial ischaemia from plaque rupture or erosion with thrombus.`,`The ECG at presentation splits it into **STEMI** and **NSTE-ACS**.`,`Troponin splits NSTE-ACS into **NSTEMI** (troponin rises) and **unstable angina** (no rise).`,`MI needs a **rise and/or fall of troponin** above the 99th percentile plus evidence of ischaemia.`,`A troponin rise alone is **myocardial injury**, not infarction.`,`Indians present 5-10 years younger, more often with STEMI and late arrival.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`STEMI: new ST elevation of **1 mm or more in two contiguous leads**.`,`In V2-V3 the cut-off is 2 mm in men 40 and over, 1.5 mm in women.`,`STEMI equivalents include **true posterior MI** and new LBBB with ischaemic symptoms.`,`Fifth UDMI 2026 uses three settings: **primary**, **secondary** and **procedure-related** MI.`,`Secondary MI is supply-demand mismatch; treat the cause and never lyse.`,`**Killip class** I to IV grades heart failure and predicts mortality.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. A thin fibrous cap ruptures, or the plaque surface erodes.`,`2. Collagen and **tissue factor** are exposed to blood.`,`3. Platelets adhere, activate through ADP and thromboxane, and aggregate.`,`4. Thrombin forms fibrin, so ACS needs **antiplatelets plus an anticoagulant**.`,`5. Occlusive **fibrin-rich red thrombus** causes STEMI, which lytics can dissolve.`,`6. Non-occlusive **platelet-rich white thrombus** causes NSTE-ACS, where lysis only harms.`,`7. Necrosis spreads from subendocardium to epicardium over **3-6 hours**: time is muscle.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Retrosternal heaviness **over 20 minutes**, not relieved by rest or nitrate.`,`It comes with sweating, nausea, breathlessness or syncope.`,`Note the **exact time of onset**; it decides reperfusion eligibility.`,`Women, elderly, diabetics and CKD patients often present atypically; give them an ECG.`,`Ask mimic questions: tearing back pain (dissection), pleuritic pain (PE), pericarditis.`,`Ask **thrombolysis contraindication** questions at once, including prior stroke and bleeding.`,`Ask about sildenafil in 24 hours or tadalafil in 48 hours before nitrates.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Record pulse, BP in both arms, SpO2; keep a **defibrillator** beside the patient.`,`Systolic under 90 with cold peripheries and confusion is **cardiogenic shock** (Killip IV).`,`Crackles and S3 assign the Killip class.`,`Hypotension, raised JVP and clear lungs in inferior MI suggest **RV infarction**.`,`Arm BP difference over 20 mmHg suggests **aortic dissection**: no lysis.`,`A new pansystolic murmur suggests septal or papillary muscle rupture.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**12-lead ECG within 10 minutes**; repeat every 15-30 minutes if non-diagnostic.`,`Add V3R-V4R in inferior MI and V7-V9 when V1-V3 show ST depression.`,`**Never delay STEMI reperfusion** waiting for troponin.`,`High-sensitivity troponin at **0 and 1 hour** rules NSTEMI in or out.`,`Baseline bloods guide dosing and bleeding risk, not the reperfusion decision.`,`Bedside echo shows wall motion, LV function and mechanical complications.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Give **aspirin 150-300 mg chewed**, then 75-100 mg daily lifelong.`,`Add a P2Y12 inhibitor: ticagrelor or prasugrel at PCI, **clopidogrel with lysis**.`,`Give oxygen **only if SpO2 is under 90%**.`,`Sublingual nitroglycerin for pain, unless hypotensive, RV infarction or PDE-5 use.`,`STEMI: primary PCI if possible within **120 minutes** of diagnosis.`,`Otherwise give **tenecteplase within 10 minutes** of diagnosis, within 12 hours of onset.`,`After lysis, transfer everyone for **angiography in 2-24 hours** (pharmacoinvasive).`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Anticoagulate with enoxaparin, or **fondaparinux 2.5 mg** in NSTE-ACS, up to 8 days.`,`Start **atorvastatin 80 mg** from day 1; target LDL under 55 mg/dL.`,`Beta-blocker when stable, clearly indicated if LVEF 40% or less.`,`ACE inhibitor within 24 hours in anterior MI, LVEF 40% or less, diabetes or hypertension.`,`Add **eplerenone** if LVEF 40% or less with heart failure or diabetes.`,`Continue DAPT for **12 months** by default; shorter if bleeding risk is high.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**Ventricular fibrillation** in the first hour is the main cause of pre-hospital death.`,`Failed lysis (ST fall under 50% at 60-90 minutes) needs **rescue PCI**, never a second lytic.`,`Inferior MI block is usually nodal and transient; give atropine 0.5 mg.`,`Anterior MI complete heart block is infranodal and needs temporary pacing.`,`RV infarction: give saline boluses and stop nitrates, morphine and diuretics.`,`Mechanical rupture peaks at **days 2-7**, when the infarct is softest.`,`Dressler syndrome at 2-6 weeks: high-dose aspirin plus colchicine.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`See the patient within 1-2 weeks of discharge; write the **DAPT stop date** on the card.`,`Check lipids at 4-6 weeks, and creatinine and potassium after ACEi or MRA changes.`,`Echo at 6-12 weeks; persisting **LVEF 35% or less** needs ICD referral.`,`Cardiac rehabilitation, tobacco cessation and yearly influenza vaccine for every patient.`,`Never stop DAPT early for minor procedures without the cardiologist.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for acute coronary syndrome`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Aspirin`,`150-300 mg chewed, then 75-100 mg daily`,`Lifelong`,`GI bleed, dyspepsia`],[`Ticagrelor`,`180 mg load, then 90 mg twice daily`,`12 months`,`Dyspnoea, bleeding; not with lysis`],[`Prasugrel`,`60 mg load, then 10 mg daily (5 mg if 75+)`,`12 months`,`Bleeding; never after stroke or TIA`],[`Clopidogrel`,`300 mg with lysis (75 mg if over 75); 600 mg before PCI`,`12 months`,`Bleeding; avoid omeprazole`],[`Tenecteplase`,`30-50 mg single IV bolus by weight`,`Once, within 12 hours`,`Intracranial bleed about 1%; half dose at 75+`],[`Streptokinase`,`1.5 million units IV over 30-60 min`,`Once only`,`Hypotension, allergy; never repeat after 5 days`],[`Enoxaparin`,`1 mg/kg SC every 12 hours`,`Up to 8 days`,`Bleeding; once daily if CrCl under 30`],[`Fondaparinux`,`2.5 mg SC once daily`,`Up to 8 days (NSTE-ACS)`,`Avoid if CrCl under 20`],[`Atorvastatin`,`80 mg oral once daily`,`Lifelong`,`Myalgia, raised transaminases`],[`Ramipril`,`1.25-2.5 mg twice daily, up to 5 mg twice daily`,`Lifelong`,`Cough, hypotension, hyperkalaemia`]]},{heading:`Tenecteplase dose by weight`,columns:[`Body weight`,`Single IV bolus`],rows:[[`Under 60 kg`,`30 mg`],[`60-69 kg`,`35 mg`],[`70-79 kg`,`40 mg`],[`80-89 kg`,`45 mg`],[`90 kg or more`,`50 mg`],[`Age 75 or over`,`Half the weight-based dose`]]},{heading:`STEMI territory on the ECG`,columns:[`Leads`,`Territory`,`Usual artery`,`Watch for`],rows:[[`II, III, aVF`,`Inferior`,`Right coronary`,`RV infarction, bradycardia, AV block`],[`V1-V4`,`Anteroseptal`,`LAD`,`Pump failure, infranodal block, LV thrombus`],[`I, aVL, V5-V6`,`Lateral`,`Circumflex`,`Subtle, easily missed`],[`ST depression V1-V3, tall R`,`Posterior`,`Circumflex or RCA`,`Record V7-V9`],[`V4R elevation`,`Right ventricle`,`Proximal RCA`,`Fluids; no nitrates or diuretics`]]},{heading:`Killip classification`,columns:[`Class`,`Findings`,`In-hospital mortality`],rows:[[`I`,`No heart failure`,`About 5%`],[`II`,`Basal crackles, S3, raised JVP`,`About 10-15%`],[`III`,`Frank pulmonary oedema`,`About 30-40%`],[`IV`,`Cardiogenic shock`,`50-80%`]]}],remember:[`ECG in 10 minutes, aspirin chewed, reperfusion decision in 10 minutes.`,`PCI not possible in 120 minutes? Lyse, then angiography in 2-24 hours.`,`Clopidogrel is the only P2Y12 inhibitor given with lytics.`,`Oxygen only if SpO2 under 90%.`,`No thrombolysis in NSTE-ACS: white thrombus, only more bleeding.`,`No nitrates in RV infarction or after PDE-5 inhibitors.`,`Mechanical complications peak at days 2-7.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`UDMI 2026 (Fifth Universal Definition): sex-specific 99th percentile troponin; troponin rise alone is injury, not infarction.`,`UDMI 2026: types 1-5 replaced by primary, secondary and procedure-related MI (vasospasm and SCAD now primary MI).`,`ACC/AHA 2025: ticagrelor monotherapy from 1 month after PCI in patients tolerating ticagrelor-based DAPT; ticagrelor/prasugrel preferred over clopidogrel.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Acute coronary syndrome`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Acute myocardial ischaemia from plaque rupture/erosion with thrombus; **MI = troponin rise/fall above sex-specific 99th percentile + ischaemia evidence** (UDMI 2026); troponin alone = injury.`],[`Relevant classification`,`By ECG: **STEMI** vs **NSTE-ACS** (NSTEMI / unstable angina); UDMI 2026 settings: **primary, secondary, procedure-related MI**; Killip I-IV; NSTE-ACS risk very high / high (GRACE over 140) / not high.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Atherothrombosis (rupture two-thirds, erosion in young/women/smokers), SCAD, embolism, spasm; tobacco, DM, HT, lipids; Indians 5-10 years younger, more STEMI and delay.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Rupture -> tissue factor + platelet adhesion/aggregation (GP IIb/IIIa) -> **occlusive fibrin-rich thrombus (STEMI)** or non-occlusive platelet thrombus (NSTE-ACS) -> necrosis wavefront over 3-6 h -> **time is muscle**.`],[`Key history`,`Pain over 20 min, not relieved by rest/nitrate, sweating, nausea; **exact onset time**; atypical in women, elderly, DM; mimics (dissection, PE, pericarditis); lysis contraindications; PDE-5 use.`],[`Key examination`,`Vitals, SpO2, **BP both arms**, Killip (crackles, S3), shock signs, new murmur (VSR, MR), **raised JVP + clear lungs + hypotension = RV infarct**; defibrillator at bedside.`],[`Investigations`,`**ECG within 10 minutes**, repeat 15-30 min; V3R-V4R, V7-V9; **hs-troponin 0/1 h**; Hb, platelets, creatinine, K, glucose; POCUS; **never delay reperfusion for troponin**.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`ST elevation at J point in 2 contiguous leads: **1 mm**; V2-V3 **2 mm men 40+, 2.5 mm men under 40, 1.5 mm women**; equivalents - posterior MI, Sgarbossa LBBB/paced, de Winter, aVR elevation + diffuse depression.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`**Aspirin 150-300 mg chewed**; PCI: **ticagrelor 180 mg** or **prasugrel 60 mg**; lysis: **clopidogrel 300 mg (75 mg if over 75)**; enoxaparin/UFH; **atorvastatin 80 mg**; SL GTN 0.4 mg (not in RV infarct, SBP under 90, PDE-5i); O2 only if SpO2 under 90%.`],[`Treatment - reperfusion`,`**Primary PCI if within 120 min of diagnosis**, else **tenecteplase within 10 min** (30-50 mg by weight, half dose 75+) or **streptokinase 1.5 MU over 30-60 min**; then angiography in 2-24 h (pharmacoinvasive).`],[`Treatment - secondary prevention`,`DAPT 12 months (ticagrelor monotherapy from 1 month allowed); beta-blocker if LVEF 40% or less; ramipril; eplerenone if LVEF 40% or less + HF/DM; LDL under 55 - ezetimibe, PCSK9i; PPI.`],[`Devices / procedures`,`Angiography **under 2 h** very high risk (shock, refractory pain, arrhythmia, AHF), **within 24 h** high risk (NSTEMI, GRACE over 140); failed lysis (ST fall under 50% at 60-90 min) = **rescue PCI**.`],[`Special groups`,`RV infarct: fluids, no nitrates/diuretics; elderly: half-dose TNK, clopidogrel 75 mg; AF: triple therapy max 1 week then DOAC + clopidogrel; cocaine ACS: no beta-blocker; secondary MI: treat cause.`],[`Complications`,`VF (first hour), heart block, cardiogenic shock, LV failure, **mechanical rupture days 2-7** (free wall, VSR, papillary), LV thrombus, pericarditis, Dressler, aneurysm.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Shock (SBP under 90 + hypoperfusion), new pansystolic murmur, failed lysis, VT/complete block, dissection signs (no lysis), bleeding on DAPT.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Review in 1-2 weeks, then 1, 3, 6, 12 months; **lipids 4-6 weeks**; creatinine/K 1-2 weeks after ACEi/MRA; **echo at 6-12 weeks - LVEF 35% or less = ICD**; write DAPT stop date.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`**STEMI India hub-and-spoke** pharmacoinvasive model; cardiac rehab, tobacco cessation, influenza vaccine, SGLT2i/GLP-1 RA in DM.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**UDMI 2026**: types 1-5 become **primary, secondary, procedure-related MI**, sex-specific troponin cut-offs; **ACC/AHA 2025**: ticagrelor/prasugrel over clopidogrel at PCI, **ticagrelor monotherapy from 1 month**; ESC 2023 remains the base.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`No lysis in NSTE-ACS; never repeat streptokinase; clopidogrel is the only P2Y12 with lytics; no nitrates in RV infarct or after PDE-5i; mechanical complications peak days 2-7.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`STEMI in a clinic without a cath lab`,steps:[{label:`Chest pain - ECG within 10 minutes`},{label:`STEMI or equivalent?`,detail:`Add V3R-V4R, V7-V9 if needed`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Load: aspirin 300 chewed, statin 80`,detail:`Monitor, defibrillator, IV line`},{label:`PCI possible within 120 min?`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Yes - transfer for primary PCI`,detail:`Ticagrelor 180 or prasugrel 60 mg`},{label:`No - lyse within 10 min of diagnosis`,detail:`TNK 30-50 mg + clopidogrel 300 (75 if 75+) + enoxaparin`,tone:`warn`},{label:`ST fall over 50% at 60-90 min?`,detail:`No - rescue PCI`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Angiography in 2-24 h for all lysed`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`After ACS: drugs every patient leaves with`,steps:[{label:`Aspirin 75-100 mg lifelong`},{label:`P2Y12 for 12 months`,detail:`Ticagrelor 90 mg BD or prasugrel 10 mg; ticagrelor alone from 1 month if tolerated; 1-3 months if high bleeding risk`},{label:`High-intensity statin`,detail:`Atorvastatin 80 mg; LDL under 55; add ezetimibe, then PCSK9i at 4-6 weeks`},{label:`Beta-blocker and ACEi`,detail:`Clearly indicated if LVEF 40% or less; ramipril 2.5 to 5 mg BD`},{label:`MRA if LVEF 40% or less with HF or DM`,detail:`Eplerenone 25-50 mg; K under 5.0`},{label:`Rehab, tobacco stop, PPI, echo at 6-12 weeks`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`UDMI 2026: three settings of MI`,columns:[`Setting`,`Mechanism`,`Management focus`],rows:[[`Primary MI`,`Acute coronary pathology - atherothrombosis, SCAD, embolism, vasospasm, late stent thrombosis`,`Antithrombotics, reperfusion as indicated`],[`Secondary MI`,`Supply-demand mismatch from another acute illness (tachyarrhythmia, anaemia, hypoxia, hypotension)`,`Treat the cause; no thrombolysis`],[`Procedure-related MI`,`Within 30 days of PCI or CABG (old types 4-5)`,`Interventional / surgical team`]]}]},"cardiovascular-heart-failure":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition, causes and key facts`,points:[`**Heart failure** is a syndrome of breathlessness, fatigue and congestion from a cardiac abnormality.`,`It also needs **raised natriuretic peptides** or objective evidence of congestion.`,`**Ischaemic heart disease** causes about 70%, then hypertension, cardiomyopathy and rheumatic disease.`,`One-year mortality is 20-30%, mainly because proven drugs are under-prescribed.`,`Reversible causes: tachycardia, alcohol, thyroid disease, severe anaemia and **thiamine deficiency**.`,`Think of **peripartum cardiomyopathy** in late pregnancy or the first months after delivery.`,`Drugs that worsen HF: **NSAIDs**, pioglitazone, verapamil and diltiazem in HFrEF.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`ESC 2026 has only two types: **HFrEF (LVEF under 50%)** and **HFpEF (50% or more)**.`,`**HFmrEF is abolished**; LVEF 41-49% is now treated as HFrEF.`,`**HFimpEF** is an LVEF rise of 10 points or more to over 40%.`,`**NYHA class I-IV** grades symptoms and changes with treatment.`,`Stages A-D describe progression and never go backwards.`,`Decompensated HF is profiled at the bedside as **warm or cold, wet or dry**.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. An index injury, such as infarction or pressure overload, reduces pumping or filling.`,`2. **Sympathetic activation** raises heart rate but chronic noradrenaline damages myocytes.`,`3. **RAAS activation** constricts arterioles and retains salt; aldosterone causes fibrosis.`,`4. The ventricle **remodels**, dilating from an ellipse into a sphere.`,`5. Left atrial pressure over 18 mmHg causes interstitial and then alveolar oedema.`,`6. Natriuretic peptides help, and sacubitril stops their breakdown by **neprilysin**.`,`7. The four drug pillars block these harmful compensations and reverse remodelling.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Grade exertional breathlessness in concrete terms to assign the **NYHA class**.`,`**Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea** is the most specific symptom of left heart failure.`,`Ask about ankle swelling, weight gain, nocturia, fatigue and falling urine output.`,`Look for the **precipitant**: missed tablets, salt, infection, AF, ischaemia or NSAIDs.`,`A weight gain of **2 kg in 3 days** precedes breathlessness.`,`Ask about syncope and palpitations, which suggest ventricular arrhythmia.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Record weight, pulse, BP lying and standing, respiratory rate and SpO2.`,`Raised **JVP** and positive hepatojugular reflux estimate high filling pressure.`,`A **third heart sound** is specific for raised filling pressure.`,`Bibasal crackles, pleural effusion, pitting oedema and tender liver show congestion.`,`Cold extremities, confusion and oliguria mean a **cold profile**; admit.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`HF is unlikely if **NT-proBNP is under 125 pg/mL** in a non-acute setting.`,`In acute breathlessness the rule-out cut-off is **NT-proBNP under 300**.`,`**Echocardiography is mandatory**; LVEF decides the class and the drugs.`,`A completely normal ECG makes HFrEF unlikely.`,`Chest X-ray shows cardiomegaly, Kerley B lines and effusions.`,`Check **iron studies in every patient**; TSAT under 20% means iron deficiency.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Teach daily weighing and a written rule for a 2 kg gain in 3 days.`,`Salt under 5 g a day; restrict fluids only in severe congestion.`,`HFrEF: start **all four foundational therapies** early at low dose.`,`The four are **ARNI (or ACEi/ARB), beta-blocker, MRA and SGLT2 inhibitor**.`,`Up-titrate every **1-2 weeks**; together they cut mortality by about 60%.`,`A **loop diuretic** relieves congestion but does not prolong life.`,`HFpEF: **SGLT2 inhibitor plus MRA** are foundational, with a diuretic for congestion.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Leave **36 hours** between the last ACE inhibitor dose and the first ARNI dose.`,`Accept creatinine rise up to 50% and potassium up to 5.5 on RAAS blockers.`,`Add **ivabradine** in sinus rhythm with rate 70 or more despite a beta-blocker.`,`Digoxin helps symptoms, especially with AF, but does not reduce mortality.`,`Give **IV ferric carboxymaltose** for iron deficiency; oral iron does not work here.`,`LVEF 35% or less after 3 months of therapy needs **ICD** referral; CRT if QRS 150 ms or more.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`HFpEF with obesity: **semaglutide or tirzepatide** can be added.`,`Pregnancy: stop ACEi, ARB, ARNI, MRA and SGLT2 inhibitors.`,`In pregnancy use metoprolol, hydralazine-nitrate and furosemide instead.`,`Diabetes with CKD: SGLT2 inhibitor plus **finerenone** reduces HF admissions.`]},{title:`Red flags, referral and follow-up`,points:[`Acute pulmonary oedema: sit up, **IV furosemide 20-40 mg**, nitrate if systolic over 110.`,`Give oxygen only if SpO2 under 90%; use CPAP or BiPAP if breathing rate is over 25.`,`**Morphine is not routine** in acute heart failure.`,`Cardiogenic shock needs noradrenaline and dobutamine, not inotropes for simple congestion.`,`Review every 1-2 weeks during titration, then every 3-6 months.`,`Check creatinine and potassium 1-2 weeks after each RAAS or MRA change.`,`Refer advanced HF for transplant, LVAD or palliative care.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for heart failure`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Sacubitril/valsartan`,`24/26 or 49/51 mg twice daily, up to 97/103 mg`,`Lifelong`,`Hypotension, hyperkalaemia; 36 h after last ACEi`],[`Enalapril`,`2.5 mg twice daily, up to 10-20 mg twice daily`,`Lifelong`,`Cough, hyperkalaemia, angioedema`],[`Bisoprolol`,`1.25 mg daily, double every 2 weeks to 10 mg`,`Lifelong`,`Fatigue, bradycardia; start when dry`],[`Carvedilol`,`3.125 mg twice daily, up to 25 mg twice daily`,`Lifelong`,`Dizziness, hypotension; take with food`],[`Spironolactone`,`12.5-25 mg daily, up to 50 mg`,`Lifelong`,`Hyperkalaemia; not if K over 5.0 or eGFR under 30`],[`Dapagliflozin or empagliflozin`,`10 mg oral once daily, no titration`,`Lifelong, any LVEF`,`Genital thrush, ketoacidosis; stop on sick days`],[`Furosemide`,`20-40 mg once or twice daily, up to 240 mg`,`Adjust to dry weight`,`Low potassium and sodium, AKI, gout`],[`Ivabradine`,`5 mg twice daily, up to 7.5 mg`,`Long term`,`Sinus rhythm only; phosphenes, bradycardia`],[`Digoxin`,`0.0625-0.125 mg daily; level 0.5-0.9 ng/mL`,`Long term`,`Toxicity with low potassium, renal failure`],[`Ferric carboxymaltose`,`500-1000 mg IV by weight and Hb`,`Repeat as needed`,`Hypophosphataemia, infusion reaction`]]},{heading:`Heart failure types (ESC 2026)`,columns:[`Type`,`LVEF`,`Treatment that improves outcome`],rows:[[`HFrEF`,`Under 50%`,`Four foundational therapies; ICD or CRT if indicated`],[`HFmrEF (abolished)`,`41-49%`,`Now treated as HFrEF`],[`HFpEF`,`50% or more`,`SGLT2 inhibitor plus MRA; diuretic; GLP-1 RA if obese`],[`HFimpEF`,`Was reduced, now over 40%`,`Continue all HFrEF drugs`]]},{heading:`NYHA functional class`,columns:[`Class`,`Limitation`,`Example`],rows:[[`I`,`None`,`Climbs stairs freely`],[`II`,`Slight; comfortable at rest`,`Breathless on one flight or uphill`],[`III`,`Marked; comfortable at rest`,`Breathless walking on level ground`],[`IV`,`Symptoms at rest`,`Breathless dressing or bathing`]]}],remember:[`Diuretics make patients feel better; the four pillars make them live longer.`,`Four pillars: ARNI, beta-blocker, MRA, SGLT2 inhibitor - start all early, low dose.`,`Only bisoprolol, carvedilol, metoprolol succinate and nebivolol are proven beta-blockers.`,`36 hours between the last ACE inhibitor and the first ARNI.`,`ESC 2026: HFmrEF is gone; MRA plus SGLT2 inhibitor at every LVEF.`,`If the EF recovers, keep all the drugs.`,`Acute HF precipitants: CHAMPIT (ACS, Hypertension, Arrhythmia, Mechanical, PE, Infection, Tamponade).`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`ESC 2026: HFmrEF abolished - HFrEF is LVEF under 50%, HFpEF 50% or more; HFimpEF = rise of 10 points or more to over 40%.`,`ESC 2026: GDMT split into foundational (ARNI/ACEi/ARB, beta-blocker, MRA, SGLT2i) and additional medical therapy.`,`ESC 2026: MRA and SGLT2 inhibitor foundational (Class I) at every LVEF, including HFpEF; GLP-1 RA (semaglutide, tirzepatide) IIa in HFpEF with obesity.`,`ESC 2026: 'acute HF' renamed decompensated HF; SGLT2 inhibitor started in hospital after stabilisation; stages A-D adopted.`,`ESC 2026: iron deficiency defined primarily by TSAT under 20%.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Heart failure`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Syndrome of **breathlessness, oedema, fatigue** from structural/functional cardiac abnormality, with **raised natriuretic peptide or objective congestion**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`ESC 2026: **HFrEF LVEF under 50%**, **HFpEF 50% or more** - HFmrEF abolished; HFimpEF (rise 10 points to over 40%); **stages A-D**; NYHA I-IV; **decompensated HF** replaces acute HF; warm/cold, wet/dry.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`IHD (~70% in India), hypertension, DCM, **rheumatic valve disease**, alcohol, peripartum, thyroid, anaemia, thiamine, constriction (TB), anthracyclines, tachycardiomyopathy.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Index injury -> low output -> **sympathetic + RAAS + ADH activation** -> vasoconstriction, Na/water retention, remodelling -> congestion and worsening pump; natriuretic peptides counter-regulate (neprilysin breaks them).`],[`Key history`,`NYHA class, **orthopnoea, PND** (most specific), oedema, weight gain **2 kg in 3 days**, cause (MI, HT, RHD, alcohol, pregnancy), precipitants, **NSAIDs/pioglitazone/verapamil**, adherence.`],[`Key examination`,`Weight, pulse, BP lying/standing, **JVP + hepatojugular reflux**, displaced apex, **S3**, crackles, effusion, oedema, hepatomegaly, perfusion (cold peripheries).`],[`Investigations`,`**NT-proBNP** (chronic under 125, acute under 300 pg/mL rules out); **echo (LVEF)**; ECG (QRS width), CXR; Na, K, creatinine, Hb, HbA1c, lipids, TSH; **iron studies - TSAT under 20%**.`],[`Diagnostic criteria`,`Symptoms/signs + raised peptide + echo abnormality; Framingham (2 major or 1 major + 2 minor) for bedside exam answers.`],[`Treatment - first line (HFrEF)`,`**Four foundational therapies** at low dose within weeks: **sacubitril/valsartan 24/26-49/51 to 97/103 mg BD** (or enalapril/ramipril), **bisoprolol 1.25 to 10 mg**, **spironolactone 12.5-25 to 50 mg**, **dapagliflozin/empagliflozin 10 mg**; titrate 1-2 weekly.`],[`Treatment - congestion and HFpEF`,`**Furosemide 20-40 mg** (to 240/day) or torsemide - symptoms only; **HFpEF: SGLT2i + MRA (spironolactone or finerenone)**, diuretic, BP/AF control, **semaglutide or tirzepatide if obese**.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Ivabradine (sinus, HR 70 or more), digoxin (0.5-0.9 ng/mL), vericiguat after worsening, hydralazine-ISDN; **IV ferric carboxymaltose** for iron deficiency; ICD if LVEF 35% or less, CRT if QRS 150 ms LBBB.`],[`How to titrate safely`,`**36 h washout ACEi -> ARNI**; creatinine/K at 1-2 weeks; accept creatinine rise 50% and K up to 5.5; MRA not if K over 5.0 or eGFR under 30; SGLT2i eGFR dip up to 30% expected; beta-blocker when dry.`],[`Special groups`,`Pregnancy/peripartum: stop ACEi/ARNI/MRA/SGLT2i - metoprolol, hydralazine-nitrate, furosemide, bromocriptine (specialist); DM + CKD: finerenone; avoid pioglitazone, saxagliptin, NSAIDs, non-DHP CCB in HFrEF.`],[`Emergency - decompensated HF`,`Sit up, O2 only if SpO2 under 90%, **IV furosemide 20-40 mg if naive (1-2x total oral daily dose if on diuretic)**, urine Na over 50-70 at 2 h, **GTN if SBP over 110**, NIV, no routine morphine; shock - noradrenaline + dobutamine; start SGLT2i before discharge.`],[`Complications`,`Arrhythmia and sudden death, AF, cardiorenal syndrome, hyperkalaemia, hyponatraemia, cachexia, thromboembolism, depression.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Pulmonary oedema, cardiogenic shock, syncope/VT with LVEF 35% or less, K over 6.0, new murmur, peripartum breathlessness, weight gain despite doubled diuretic.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`1-2 weekly during titration (STRONG-HF: full doses within 2 weeks of discharge), then 3-6 monthly; **echo after 3 months OMT** for ICD/CRT; **HFimpEF - never withdraw drugs** (TRED-HF).`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Control HT, DM (SGLT2i), CAD, alcohol; daily weights, salt under 5 g, influenza/pneumococcal vaccine, cardiac rehab, palliative care in advanced HF.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**ESC 2026**: two phenotypes (cut-off 50%), foundational vs additional therapy, MRA + SGLT2i Class I at any LVEF, GLP-1 RA in obese HFpEF, decompensated HF, TSAT-based iron deficiency.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Diuretics relieve, foundational drugs prolong life; only bisoprolol, carvedilol, metoprolol succinate, nebivolol; ivabradine useless in AF; BNP (not NT-proBNP) rises on ARNI.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Suspected heart failure: diagnosis to phenotype (ESC 2026)`,steps:[{label:`Breathlessness, oedema, fatigue`,detail:`History, exam, ECG`},{label:`NT-proBNP 125 pg/mL or more?`,detail:`Acute: 300 or more`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Below cut-off - HF unlikely, look elsewhere`},{label:`Echocardiography: measure LVEF`,tone:`decision`},{label:`LVEF under 50% - HFrEF`,detail:`Includes old HFmrEF 41-49%`,tone:`warn`},{label:`LVEF 50% or more + structural/diastolic change - HFpEF`},{label:`Find the cause and start foundational therapy`,detail:`Ischaemia, valves, HT, alcohol, peripartum, thyroid`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`HFrEF: foundational then additional therapy`,steps:[{label:`Start all four foundational drugs early at low dose`,detail:`Sacubitril/valsartan 24/26-49/51 BD, bisoprolol 1.25, spironolactone 12.5-25, dapagliflozin 10 mg`},{label:`Titrate every 1-2 weeks to target`,detail:`ARNI 97/103 BD, bisoprolol 10 mg; creatinine and K at 1-2 weeks`},{label:`Diuretic to dry weight`,detail:`Furosemide 20-40 mg; flexible by daily weight`},{label:`Additional therapy if still symptomatic`,detail:`Ivabradine if sinus HR 70+; IV iron if TSAT under 20%; vericiguat; digoxin; H-ISDN`},{label:`Devices at 3 months of OMT`,detail:`LVEF 35% or less - ICD; QRS 150 ms LBBB - CRT`,tone:`good`},{label:`Advanced HF - LVAD, transplant, palliation`,tone:`warn`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`HFrEF versus HFpEF after ESC 2026`,columns:[`Feature`,`HFrEF`,`HFpEF`],rows:[[`LVEF`,`Under 50%`,`50% or more`],[`Typical patient`,`Post-MI, DCM, younger men`,`Elderly, women, HT, obesity, DM, AF`],[`Foundational drugs`,`ARNI/ACEi/ARB + beta-blocker + MRA + SGLT2i`,`SGLT2i + MRA`],[`Extra options`,`Ivabradine, vericiguat, digoxin, ICD/CRT`,`GLP-1 RA if obese (IIa), diuretic, BP/AF control`]]}]},"cardiovascular-dyslipidaemia":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Dyslipidaemia** is any abnormal plasma lipid level that raises atherosclerotic risk.`,`Cut-offs: LDL 130, triglycerides 150, non-HDL 160 mg/dL or more.`,`Low HDL is under 40 mg/dL in men and under 50 mg/dL in women.`,`About **81% of Indian adults** have some dyslipidaemia; low HDL is commonest.`,`The Indian pattern is **atherogenic**: high triglycerides, low HDL and small dense LDL.`,`**Non-HDL** is total minus HDL; its target is always LDL target plus 30.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Treatment follows the **risk category**, not the lipid number.`,`LAI 2023 categories: low, moderate, high, very high, extreme A and extreme B.`,`Any established ASCVD puts the patient straight into **very high risk**.`,`**Primary** causes are genetic, such as familial hypercholesterolaemia (about 1 in 250).`,`**Secondary** causes: diabetes, hypothyroidism, nephrotic syndrome, cholestasis, alcohol and drugs.`,`Fredrickson types I-V are still asked but no longer guide treatment.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. The liver secretes VLDL, which lipoprotein lipase turns into **LDL**.`,`2. LDL is cleared by the **hepatic LDL receptor**.`,`3. **PCSK9** destroys LDL receptors, so more PCSK9 means higher LDL.`,`4. LDL is trapped in the intima, oxidised and eaten by macrophages to form **foam cells**.`,`5. A fibrous cap forms; a thin cap ruptures and causes ACS or stroke.`,`6. Insulin resistance over-produces VLDL, giving high triglycerides and low HDL.`,`7. Triglycerides over 1000 mg/dL cause **acute pancreatitis** and eruptive xanthomas.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Most patients have no symptoms, so the history is a **risk assessment**.`,`Ask about angina, MI, stroke or claudication; any one means very high risk.`,`Record diabetes, hypertension, tobacco and CKD.`,`Ask about **premature coronary disease** in parents and siblings, a pointer to FH.`,`Ask about symptoms of hypothyroidism and nephrotic syndrome, and alcohol intake.`,`If a statin was stopped before, ask what happened; most intolerance is nocebo.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Measure BMI and waist; **90 cm in men and 80 cm in women** is central obesity.`,`**Tendon xanthomas** are almost diagnostic of FH; feel Achilles and knuckle tendons.`,`**Corneal arcus before 45** suggests FH; xanthelasma is non-specific.`,`**Eruptive xanthomas** mean triglycerides usually above 1000 mg/dL.`,`Palmar crease xanthomas point to type III dysbetalipoproteinaemia.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**Non-fasting lipid profile** is fine for screening and follow-up.`,`Fast only if triglycerides are above 400; Friedewald LDL is then invalid.`,`Screen once for secondary causes: **TSH**, HbA1c, creatinine, ALT and urine protein.`,`Measure **lipoprotein(a) once** in every adult; 50 mg/dL or more raises risk.`,`Check CK only for muscle symptoms; routine CK is not needed.`,`CAC score 100 or more reclassifies intermediate risk to high risk.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Lifestyle trial of about 3 months only in low and moderate risk.`,`Start a statin at once in ASCVD, diabetes over 40, CKD, or LDL 190 or more.`,`Cut saturated and trans fat: ghee, vanaspati, palm oil and bakery items.`,`For high triglycerides, stop alcohol, cut sugar and lose 5-10% weight.`,`**Statins are first line**: atorvastatin 40-80 mg or rosuvastatin 20-40 mg for high intensity.`,`Start rosuvastatin at **5-10 mg in Asians**, because blood levels are higher.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Doubling a statin dose adds only **about 6%** more LDL lowering.`,`First add-on is **ezetimibe 10 mg**, lowering LDL a further 20-25%.`,`Then add bempedoic acid, a **PCSK9 antibody** or inclisiran.`,`Triglycerides 500 or more: **fenofibrate** to prevent pancreatitis.`,`Never combine a statin with **gemfibrozil**; fenofibrate is the fibrate to pair.`,`Icosapent ethyl 2 g twice daily cuts events; ordinary fish oil does not.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`Diabetes aged 40-75: a statin for all, at least moderate intensity.`,`Do not start a statin in patients **already on dialysis**, but continue an existing one.`,`Stop statins **1-3 months before planned pregnancy** and during breastfeeding.`,`Children with FH: statin from about **8-10 years** of age.`,`FH: score with the **Dutch Lipid Clinic Network** criteria and screen all first-degree relatives.`]},{title:`Red flags, referral and follow-up`,points:[`Muscle pain with dark urine or CK over 10 times normal means **rhabdomyolysis**; stop the statin.`,`CK under 4 times normal: stop 2-4 weeks, then rechallenge at a lower dose.`,`ALT over 3 times normal on two readings: stop or reduce the statin.`,`Triglyceride pancreatitis: nil by mouth, IV fluids and **IV insulin infusion** with dextrose.`,`Recheck lipids **4-12 weeks** after any change, then every 6-12 months.`,`Aim for the target and at least a **50% fall** in high and very high risk.`,`Refer suspected FH, LDL above target on maximal oral therapy, or triglycerides over 1000.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Lipid-lowering drugs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Atorvastatin`,`10-20 mg (moderate) or 40-80 mg (high) daily`,`Lifelong`,`Myalgia, raised ALT; CYP3A4 interactions`],[`Rosuvastatin`,`5-10 mg (moderate) or 20-40 mg (high) daily`,`Lifelong`,`Myalgia; max 10 mg if eGFR under 30`],[`Simvastatin`,`20-40 mg at night`,`Lifelong`,`Myopathy; max 20 mg with amlodipine`],[`Ezetimibe`,`10 mg oral once daily`,`Lifelong`,`Rare diarrhoea; first add-on`],[`Bempedoic acid`,`180 mg oral once daily`,`Lifelong`,`Raised uric acid, gout`],[`Evolocumab`,`140 mg SC every 2 weeks or 420 mg monthly`,`Long term`,`Injection-site reaction`],[`Inclisiran`,`284 mg SC day 0, 3 months, then 6-monthly`,`Long term`,`Injection-site reaction`],[`Fenofibrate`,`145 mg or 160 mg daily with food`,`Long term`,`Creatinine rise, gallstones; avoid eGFR under 30`],[`Icosapent ethyl`,`2 g twice daily with meals`,`Long term, on a statin`,`AF, bleeding`],[`Cholestyramine`,`4-8 g once or twice daily, max 24 g`,`As needed`,`Constipation, binds drugs; safe in pregnancy`]]},{heading:`LAI 2023 risk categories and targets`,columns:[`Risk category`,`Example`,`LDL target (mg/dL)`,`Non-HDL target (mg/dL)`],rows:[[`Low or moderate`,`0-2 major risk factors`,`Under 100`,`Under 130`],[`High`,`3+ risk factors, FH, LDL 190+, Lp(a) 50+`,`Under 70`,`Under 100`],[`Very high`,`ASCVD; diabetes with organ damage`,`Under 50`,`Under 80`],[`Extreme A`,`CAD with diabetes, FH or polyvascular disease`,`Under 50 (optional 30)`,`Under 80`],[`Extreme B`,`Recurrent ACS despite LDL under 50`,`30 or less`,`60 or less`]]},{heading:`Statin intensity`,columns:[`Intensity`,`LDL fall`,`Drugs and doses`],rows:[[`High`,`50% or more`,`Atorvastatin 40-80 mg, rosuvastatin 20-40 mg`],[`Moderate`,`30-49%`,`Atorvastatin 10-20, rosuvastatin 5-10, simvastatin 20-40 mg`],[`Add-on`,`Further 20-25%`,`Ezetimibe 10 mg`],[`Add-on`,`Further 50-60%`,`PCSK9 antibody or inclisiran`]]}],remember:[`Treat the risk, not the number.`,`Ladder: statin, then ezetimibe, then bempedoic acid or PCSK9 agent.`,`Non-HDL target = LDL target plus 30, no fasting needed.`,`LAI LDL targets: 100, 70, 50, 30.`,`Fenofibrate with a statin is fine; gemfibrozil with a statin is not.`,`Check TSH before blaming a statin for myalgia.`,`Tendon xanthomas mean FH: cascade-screen every first-degree relative.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`ACC/AHA 2026 dyslipidemia guideline replaces 2018: LDL goals return (under 100, under 70 high PREVENT risk, under 55 very high-risk ASCVD); PREVENT replaces pooled cohort equations.`,`ACC/AHA 2026: Lp(a) at least once in every adult with cascade testing; over 125 nmol/L about 1.4-fold, over 250 nmol/L 2-fold risk.`,`ACC/AHA 2026: adult lipid screening from 19 years every 5 years and universal child screening at 9-11 years.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Dyslipidaemia`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Abnormal lipids raising ASCVD risk: **LDL 130 or more, TG 150 or more, HDL under 40 (M)/50 (F), non-HDL 160 or more mg/dL**; Indian phenotype - **atherogenic (high TG, low HDL, small dense LDL)**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**LAI 2023 risk categories**: low, moderate, high, very high, extreme A, extreme B; primary (FH, FCHL) vs secondary; Fredrickson I-V for short notes only.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Genetic (FH 1 in 250 - LDLR, APOB, PCSK9); secondary - **diabetes, hypothyroidism, nephrotic syndrome, cholestasis, alcohol**, drugs (thiazides, steroids, PIs, retinoids).`],[`Pathophysiology`,`ApoB particles retained in intima -> oxidised LDL -> macrophage foam cells -> fibroatheroma -> rupture -> ACS/stroke; **PCSK9 degrades LDL receptor**; TG over 1000 -> **pancreatitis**.`],[`Key history`,`Established ASCVD, DM, HT, CKD, tobacco; **family history of premature CAD/FH**; secondary-cause symptoms; diet (ghee, vanaspati); past statin symptoms (nocebo); pregnancy plans; interacting drugs.`],[`Key examination`,`BMI, waist 90/80 cm, BP, acanthosis; **tendon xanthomas (FH)**, arcus under 45, eruptive xanthomas (TG over 1000), palmar crease xanthomas (type III), lipaemia retinalis; ABI.`],[`Investigations`,`Non-fasting lipid profile (fast if TG over 400; Friedewald invalid - Martin-Hopkins/direct LDL); **TSH, HbA1c, creatinine, ALT**; **Lp(a) once in every adult** (50 mg/dL or more = risk enhancer).`],[`Diagnostic criteria (risk category)`,`Place in LAI category -> target; ASCVD = very high without scoring; CAC 100 or more reclassifies up; **Dutch Lipid Clinic score over 8 = definite FH**; ACC/AHA 2026 uses PREVENT.`],[`Treatment targets`,`**LAI**: LDL under 100 (low-moderate), under 70 (high), **under 50 (very high, extreme A)**, **30 or less (extreme B)**; non-HDL = LDL + 30; ESC/ACC-AHA very high risk **under 55 + 50% fall**.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Lifestyle (sat fat under 7%, no trans fat, fibre, exercise); **statin**: high intensity **atorvastatin 40-80 / rosuvastatin 20-40 mg**, moderate atorva 10-20 / rosuva 5-10 mg; any time (simvastatin at night).`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`**Ezetimibe 10 mg** -> **bempedoic acid 180 mg** or **PCSK9 mAb (evolocumab 140 mg q2w)** / **inclisiran 284 mg day 0, 3 months, 6-monthly**; very high risk far from target - statin + ezetimibe together.`],[`Treatment - triglycerides`,`150-499: lifestyle, glucose, get LDL/non-HDL to target; **icosapent ethyl 2 g BD** if high risk; **500 or more: fenofibrate 145/160 mg** + omega-3, no alcohol; never gemfibrozil with statin.`],[`Special groups`,`Pregnancy: stop statins 1-3 months before conception (bile acid sequestrants only); CKD: statin +/- ezetimibe, don't start on dialysis; FH child: statin from 8-10 y; HIV: pitavastatin.`],[`Complications (drug adverse effects)`,`Myalgia: CK + TSH, stop 2-4 weeks, rechallenge lower/alternate-day; **CK over 10x = rhabdomyolysis**; ALT over 3x stop; slight new diabetes risk - continue; rosuvastatin start 5-10 mg in Asians.`],[`Emergency`,`**Hypertriglyceridaemic pancreatitis** (TG over 1000): NBM, fluids, **IV insulin 0.1-0.3 U/kg/h + dextrose**, plasmapheresis if refractory; statin rhabdomyolysis - stop, IV fluids.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`TG over 1000 with abdominal pain; LDL 190 or more or tendon xanthomas (FH - cascade screen); LDL over 500 in child (HoFH); dark urine on statin; above target on maximal oral therapy.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Lipids **4-12 weeks** after change, then 6-12 monthly; check adherence before escalating; low LDL on treatment is safe.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Adults from 18-20 every 5 years; **children 9-11 years universally**, from 2 years if FH/premature ASCVD in parent; cascade screening of FH relatives.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**ACC/AHA 2026**: LDL goals return (under 55 in very high-risk ASCVD), PREVENT replaces PCE, universal Lp(a) once; ESC/EAS 2025 focused update; LAI 2023 remains the Indian standard.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Treat the risk, not the number; each statin dose doubling adds ~6% LDL fall; Lp(a) not lowered by statins; xanthelasma is non-specific, tendon xanthoma means FH.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Abnormal lipids: from report to target`,steps:[{label:`Lipid profile (non-fasting)`,detail:`Fast only if TG over 400`},{label:`TG over 1000 mg/dL?`,detail:`Pancreatitis risk - fenofibrate, no alcohol, urgent`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Exclude secondary causes`,detail:`TSH, HbA1c, creatinine, ALT, urine protein, drugs`},{label:`Assign LAI risk category`,detail:`ASCVD = very high; DLCN for FH; Lp(a), CAC if uncertain`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Set LDL and non-HDL target`,detail:`100 / 70 / 50 / 30 mg/dL; non-HDL = LDL + 30`},{label:`Start statin intensity to match`,detail:`Lifestyle trial 3 months only in low-moderate risk`},{label:`Recheck at 4-12 weeks - at target`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`LDL-lowering escalation`,steps:[{label:`Maximal tolerated statin`,detail:`Atorvastatin 40-80 or rosuvastatin 20-40 mg; ~50% fall`},{label:`Add ezetimibe 10 mg`,detail:`Further 20-25%; start together if far from target`},{label:`Statin intolerance: bempedoic acid 180 mg`,detail:`17-25% fall; gout risk`},{label:`PCSK9 mAb or inclisiran`,detail:`Evolocumab 140 mg SC q2w / inclisiran 284 mg day 0, 3 months, then 6-monthly; 50-60% fall`},{label:`LDL at target - continue lifelong`,tone:`good`}]}]},"cardiovascular-atrial-fibrillation":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition, causes and key facts`,points:[`**Atrial fibrillation** is chaotic atrial activity with loss of effective atrial contraction.`,`ECG shows **no P waves** and **irregularly irregular** R-R intervals.`,`Diagnosis needs a 12-lead ECG or a single-lead tracing of **30 seconds or more**.`,`AF raises stroke risk about **fivefold**.`,`In India patients are a decade younger, and **rheumatic heart disease** remains a leading cause.`,`Always look for **thyrotoxicosis** and sleep apnoea.`,`Obesity, diabetes and ageing enlarge and scar the atrium.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Paroxysmal**: ends by itself or with treatment within 7 days.`,`**Persistent**: lasts over 7 days; long-standing persistent is 12 months or more.`,`**Permanent**: patient and doctor accept AF and stop rhythm control.`,`Stroke risk does **not depend on the type**; paroxysmal AF is anticoagulated the same way.`,`**Valvular AF** means only moderate-severe mitral stenosis or a mechanical valve.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Ectopic beats fire from the **pulmonary veins** into the left atrium.`,`2. A stretched, scarred atrium sustains multiple re-entrant wavelets.`,`3. **AF begets AF**: fast rates shorten the refractory period and cause fibrosis.`,`4. The AV node filters impulses, giving an irregularly irregular ventricular rate.`,`5. Loss of atrial kick reduces filling, worst in mitral stenosis and HFpEF.`,`6. Stasis in the **left atrial appendage** forms clot that embolises to the brain.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask about irregular palpitations, breathlessness, fatigue and reduced effort tolerance.`,`Many patients have no symptoms; AF is found on a routine pulse.`,`Grade symptoms on the **modified EHRA scale**.`,`Ask the CHA2DS2-VA items: heart failure, hypertension, age, diabetes, stroke, vascular disease.`,`Ask about **rheumatic fever** or known valve disease; this changes the anticoagulant.`,`Take a bleeding history and list NSAIDs, antiplatelets and alcohol.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Feel the pulse for a full minute; any irregular pulse needs an ECG.`,`Count apical and radial rates together to find the **pulse deficit**.`,`The JVP has **no a wave**; S1 varies in intensity.`,`Listen for **mitral stenosis**: loud S1, opening snap and mid-diastolic rumble.`,`Look for heart failure, thyroid signs and neurological deficit.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**12-lead ECG** confirms AF and shows rate, LVH and pre-excitation.`,`**Echocardiography** in every new AF, to find mitral stenosis and check LVEF.`,`Bloods: **TSH**, haemoglobin, creatinine clearance, liver function, electrolytes and INR.`,`Holter monitoring for suspected paroxysmal AF and after unexplained stroke.`,`TOE only before early cardioversion, to exclude appendage clot.`,`Screen everyone **65 or older** by pulse or single-lead ECG.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Anticoagulate if **CHA2DS2-VA is 2 or more**; consider at 1.`,`Choose a **DOAC over warfarin**, except in mitral stenosis or mechanical valve.`,`**Aspirin is not an alternative** to anticoagulation.`,`Dose DOACs by **Cockcroft-Gault creatinine clearance**, not eGFR.`,`Rate control starts with a lenient target: **resting rate under 110**.`,`**Beta-blockers** are first line at any LVEF; diltiazem only if LVEF over 40%.`,`Treat comorbidities: BP, weight loss, less alcohol and sleep apnoea.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Add **digoxin** in heart failure or sedentary patients; keep level under 1.2 ng/mL.`,`Offer **rhythm control** to symptomatic patients, ideally within 12 months of diagnosis.`,`**Flecainide** or propafenone only in a structurally normal heart.`,`**Amiodarone** is used with structural heart disease or heart failure.`,`Catheter ablation is a first-line option for symptomatic paroxysmal AF.`,`Anticoagulation continues by score, even after successful ablation.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`Warfarin target **INR 2.0-3.0**; 2.5-3.5 for mechanical mitral valve.`,`Warfarin is teratogenic; switch to LMWH in pregnancy.`,`AF with a coronary stent: triple therapy up to **1 week**, then DOAC plus clopidogrel.`,`After ischaemic stroke, start a DOAC early: within 48 hours if minor or moderate.`]},{title:`Red flags, referral and follow-up`,points:[`Unstable AF needs **immediate synchronised DC cardioversion**, whatever the duration.`,`AF over 24 hours: 3 weeks of anticoagulation or TOE before cardioversion.`,`Anticoagulate for at least **4 weeks after** any cardioversion.`,`**Pre-excited AF**: never give adenosine, digoxin, verapamil or diltiazem.`,`Major bleed on warfarin: **vitamin K 5-10 mg IV** plus prothrombin complex concentrate.`,`Idarucizumab reverses dabigatran; andexanet alfa reverses factor Xa inhibitors.`,`Refer mitral stenosis, HFrEF, pre-excitation and symptoms despite rate control.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for atrial fibrillation`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Apixaban`,`5 mg twice daily (2.5 mg if 2 of 3 criteria)`,`Long term while score indicates`,`Bleeding; avoid CrCl under 15`],[`Rivaroxaban`,`20 mg daily with evening meal (15 mg if CrCl 15-49)`,`Long term`,`Bleeding; must take with food`],[`Dabigatran`,`150 mg twice daily (110 mg if 80+ or verapamil)`,`Long term`,`Dyspepsia; avoid CrCl under 30`],[`Edoxaban`,`60 mg daily (30 mg if any criterion)`,`Long term`,`Bleeding; avoid CrCl under 15`],[`Warfarin`,`2.5-5 mg each evening, to INR 2-3`,`Long term`,`Bleeding, many interactions; teratogenic`],[`Metoprolol succinate`,`25-200 mg oral once daily`,`Long term`,`Bradycardia, bronchospasm`],[`Diltiazem`,`60 mg three times daily or SR 120-360 mg`,`Long term`,`Only if LVEF over 40%; hypotension`],[`Digoxin`,`0.0625-0.25 mg once daily`,`Long term`,`Toxicity with low K; level under 1.2`],[`Amiodarone`,`200 mg three times daily, tapering to 200 mg daily`,`Long term`,`Thyroid, lung, liver; check TSH, LFT 6-monthly`],[`Flecainide`,`50-150 mg twice daily; pill-in-pocket 200-300 mg`,`As needed`,`Normal heart only; give with beta-blocker`]]},{heading:`CHA2DS2-VA score (ESC 2024)`,columns:[`Letter`,`Meaning`,`Points`],rows:[[`C`,`Chronic heart failure or LVEF 40% or less`,`1`],[`H`,`Hypertension`,`1`],[`A2`,`Age 75 or more`,`2`],[`D`,`Diabetes`,`1`],[`S2`,`Prior stroke, TIA or thromboembolism`,`2`],[`V`,`Vascular disease (MI, PAD, aortic plaque)`,`1`],[`A`,`Age 65-74`,`1`]]},{heading:`Rate control or rhythm: which drug when`,columns:[`Situation`,`Preferred choice`,`Avoid`],rows:[[`LVEF over 40%`,`Beta-blocker or diltiazem or verapamil`,`None specific`],[`LVEF 40% or less`,`Beta-blocker, add digoxin`,`Diltiazem, verapamil, dronedarone`],[`Normal heart, rhythm control`,`Flecainide or propafenone`,`Use in scarred ventricle`],[`Structural heart disease`,`Amiodarone`,`Class IC drugs`],[`Pre-excited AF (WPW)`,`DC shock or procainamide`,`All AV nodal blockers`]]}],remember:[`CHA2DS2-VA: anticoagulate at 2, consider at 1, same for men and women.`,`Mitral stenosis or mechanical valve: warfarin, never a DOAC.`,`High HAS-BLED means fix bleeding risks, never withhold anticoagulation.`,`Aspirin is not a substitute for anticoagulation.`,`Cardioversion: 24-hour rule, then 4 weeks of anticoagulation afterwards.`,`Rivaroxaban with food; dabigatran capsules never opened.`,`Every new AF: TSH and echo.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`AHA 2025 ACLS: synchronised cardioversion of AF - first shock 200 J or more biphasic (replaces 120-200 J).`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Atrial fibrillation`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Supraventricular arrhythmia with uncoordinated atrial activation: **no P waves, irregularly irregular RR**, documented on 12-lead ECG or **30 s single-lead strip**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**First-diagnosed, paroxysmal (under 7 days), persistent (over 7 days), long-standing persistent (12 months or more), permanent**; valvular = moderate-severe MS or mechanical valve; EHRA symptom score.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`HT, IHD, HF, **rheumatic MS (India)**, thyrotoxicosis, alcohol (holiday heart), OSA, obesity, DM, age, PE, pneumonia, post-surgery.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Pulmonary vein triggers + fibrotic atrial substrate -> re-entrant wavelets -> **AF begets AF**; loss of atrial kick; **LAA stasis -> thrombus -> cardioembolic stroke (5x risk)**.`],[`Key history`,`Palpitations, breathlessness, syncope (tachy-brady, pre-excitation), **CHA2DS2-VA items**, rheumatic fever, thyroid symptoms, alcohol, snoring, bleeding history, interacting drugs, cost/INR access.`],[`Key examination`,`Irregularly irregular pulse, **apex-radial deficit**, absent a wave, variable S1, **MS murmur**, HF signs, thyroid signs, neuro screen, bleeding/anaemia.`],[`Investigations`,`**12-lead ECG**, **echo in every new AF** (MS, LVEF, LA size), **TSH**, Hb, **Cockcroft-Gault CrCl**, LFT, electrolytes, INR; Holter for paroxysmal; TOE before early cardioversion.`],[`Stroke risk classification (CHA2DS2-VA)`,`C HF 1, H HT 1, **A2 age 75+ 2**, D DM 1, **S2 stroke/TIA 2**, V vascular 1, A 65-74 1; **2 or more - anticoagulate; 1 - consider**; no sex point (ESC 2024).`],[`Treatment - first line (anticoagulation)`,`**DOAC over warfarin**: apixaban 5 mg BD (2.5 if 2 of age 80+, wt 60 kg or less, creat 1.5+), rivaroxaban 20 mg OD with food, dabigatran 150 BD, edoxaban 60 OD; **warfarin INR 2-3 in MS/mechanical valve**.`],[`Treatment - rate control`,`Lenient **resting rate under 110**; beta-blocker any LVEF (metoprolol succinate 25-200 mg); diltiazem/verapamil only if **LVEF over 40%**; digoxin add-on (level under 1.2 ng/mL).`],[`Devices / procedures (rhythm control)`,`Early rhythm control within 12 months if CV risk (EAST-AFNET 4); flecainide/propafenone in normal heart; amiodarone in structural disease; **PVI ablation** first-line option in paroxysmal; LAA occlusion if OAC impossible.`],[`Treatment - cardioversion`,`Unstable: **synchronised DC 200 J or more biphasic** now; stable under 24 h - cardiovert after anticoagulation; 24 h or more/unknown - **3 weeks OAC or TOE**; OAC **4 weeks after** all cardioversions.`],[`Special groups`,`Rheumatic MS/mechanical valve: **warfarin, never DOAC** (INVICTUS); pregnancy: LMWH; CrCl under 30 no dabigatran, under 15 no Xa inhibitor; post-stent: triple max 1 week then DOAC + clopidogrel to 12 months.`],[`Complications`,`Stroke/systemic embolism, tachycardiomyopathy, HF, bleeding on OAC, dementia; pre-excited AF -> VF.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Haemodynamic instability, **pre-excited AF (no AV nodal blockers)**, stroke/TIA, limb or mesenteric embolism, major bleeding, rate over 150 with symptoms, MS.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`AF-CARE 'E': review at 6 months then yearly; DOAC - Hb, CrCl, LFT yearly (CrCl/10 months if under 60); warfarin INR 4-weekly, TTR over 70%; missed DOAC: 6 h (BD) / 12 h (OD) rule.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`BP 120-129, **weight loss 10%**, alcohol 3 drinks/week or less, exercise, OSA/DM/thyroid treatment; opportunistic pulse/ECG screening at 65+.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**ESC 2024**: **AF-CARE** (Comorbidity, Avoid stroke, Reduce symptoms, Evaluate), **CHA2DS2-VA** (no sex point), cardioversion threshold now **24 h**, early rhythm control; **AHA 2025**: AF cardioversion first shock 200 J or more.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Aspirin is no substitute for OAC; HAS-BLED never withholds OAC; dose DOACs by Cockcroft-Gault; ivabradine and AV nodal blockers useless/dangerous in pre-excited AF; check TSH and echo in all.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`New AF in the clinic: the AF-CARE sequence`,steps:[{label:`Irregular pulse - confirm on ECG`,detail:`No P waves, irregular RR; 30 s strip`},{label:`Haemodynamically unstable?`,detail:`SBP under 90, pulmonary oedema, ischaemia, confusion`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Yes - synchronised DC cardioversion`,detail:`First shock 200 J or more biphasic, heparin`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Stable - echo, TSH, CrCl, Hb`,detail:`MS or mechanical valve changes the drug`},{label:`CHA2DS2-VA 2 or more?`,detail:`1 - consider; 0 - none`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Anticoagulate: DOAC (warfarin in MS)`,detail:`Fix modifiable bleeding risks`},{label:`Rate control under 110 + treat comorbidity`,detail:`Consider early rhythm control`},{label:`Dynamic review at 6 months, then yearly`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Rate control by LVEF`,steps:[{label:`Beta-blocker (any LVEF)`,detail:`Metoprolol succinate 25-200 mg or bisoprolol 1.25-10 mg`},{label:`LVEF over 40%: diltiazem or verapamil alternative`,detail:`Diltiazem SR 120-360 mg/day; avoid in LVEF 40% or less`},{label:`Add digoxin if still fast or sedentary/HF`,detail:`0.0625-0.25 mg OD; level under 1.2 ng/mL; watch K`},{label:`Acute with HF: IV amiodarone or digoxin`,detail:`Amiodarone 300 mg over 1 h then 900 mg/24 h`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Refractory: ablate-and-pace or rhythm control`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`DOAC dosing at a glance`,columns:[`DOAC`,`Standard`,`Reduced`,`Avoid if CrCl`],rows:[[`Apixaban`,`5 mg BD`,`2.5 mg BD if 2 of: age 80+, wt 60 kg or less, creat 1.5+`,`Under 15`],[`Rivaroxaban`,`20 mg OD with evening meal`,`15 mg OD if CrCl 15-49`,`Under 15`],[`Dabigatran`,`150 mg BD`,`110 mg BD if 80+ or verapamil`,`Under 30`],[`Edoxaban`,`60 mg OD`,`30 mg if CrCl 15-50, wt 60 kg or less, P-gp inhibitor`,`Under 15`]]}]},"cardiovascular-ecg-interpretation":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`The **ECG** records the heart's electrical activity from 12 viewpoints.`,`Standard calibration is **25 mm/s and 10 mm/mV**.`,`One small square is **0.04 s**; one large square is **0.20 s**.`,`Normal rate is 60-100, PR **0.12-0.20 s**, QRS **under 0.12 s**.`,`Normal QTc is under 450 ms in men and 460 ms in women.`,`Normal axis is **-30 to +90 degrees**.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Inferior leads are **II, III and aVF**; high lateral leads are I and aVL.`,`Septal leads are V1-V2, anterior V3-V4 and lateral V5-V6.`,`Group abnormalities as rate and rhythm, conduction blocks and chamber enlargement.`,`Then ischaemia and infarction, and metabolic, drug and channelopathy patterns.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. The **SA node** fires and atrial depolarisation makes the P wave.`,`2. The **AV node** delays the impulse, forming most of the PR interval.`,`3. Fast His-Purkinje conduction gives a narrow QRS; a blocked branch widens it.`,`4. The ST segment is the plateau phase, so it is normally **isoelectric**.`,`5. **Subendocardial ischaemia** gives ST depression, which does not localise.`,`6. **Transmural injury** gives ST elevation in facing leads and reciprocal depression opposite.`,`7. Dead muscle is electrically silent, so a **pathological Q wave** appears.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Record an ECG **within 10 minutes** for chest pain, syncope, breathlessness or palpitations.`,`Note whether the patient had **pain during the recording**.`,`Ask about exertional syncope and sudden death under 40 in the family.`,`List **QT-prolonging drugs**: macrolides, quinolones, antipsychotics, ondansetron and hydroxychloroquine.`,`Ask about CKD, vomiting and diuretics, which upset potassium and magnesium.`,`Always ask for **old ECGs** to compare.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Place **V1 and V2 in the 4th intercostal space**; too high mimics septal infarct.`,`Place V4 in the 5th space at the mid-clavicular line.`,`**Arm lead reversal** inverts lead I but keeps V6 normal.`,`Tremor can mimic AF or VF; **never treat an artefact**, feel the pulse.`,`Record **V4R in every inferior STEMI** and V7-V9 when V1-V3 show ST depression.`]},{title:`Diagnosis - the systematic reading`,points:[`Rate: **300 divided by large squares** between R waves.`,`For irregular rhythm, count QRS complexes in 10 seconds and multiply by 6.`,`Rhythm: is it regular, is there a P before every QRS, is QRS narrow or broad?`,`Axis: leads I and aVF both positive means **normal axis**.`,`Measure PR, QRS and **QTc (QT divided by square root of RR)**.`,`LVH: **Sokolow-Lyon 35 mm or more**; look for strain pattern.`,`Finish with one line: rhythm, rate, axis, key finding and action now.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`High-sensitivity troponin at 0 and 1 hour diagnoses NSTEMI.`,`**Do not wait for troponin** when the ECG shows STEMI.`,`Check potassium, magnesium and calcium for peaked T, U waves or long QT.`,`Echo for LVH, bundle branch block, Q waves or low voltage.`,`Holter or event recorder for intermittent palpitations or syncope.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`STEMI: **aspirin 150-300 mg chewed**, P2Y12 load and atorvastatin 80 mg.`,`Primary PCI if possible **within 120 minutes**, otherwise lysis within 10 minutes.`,`Hyperkalaemia with ECG changes: **10% calcium gluconate 30 mL IV** before the result.`,`Then give 10 units insulin with 25 g glucose and nebulised salbutamol.`,`Narrow regular tachycardia: Valsalva, then **adenosine 6 mg, 12 mg, 18 mg** rapid IV.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Unstable tachycardia: **synchronised DC cardioversion**, whatever the rhythm.`,`Stable broad-complex tachycardia: **amiodarone 300 mg IV** over 10-20 minutes.`,`Bradycardia: **atropine 500 microgram IV**, repeated to 3 mg.`,`No response: transcutaneous pacing or adrenaline infusion.`,`Torsades: **magnesium sulphate 2 g IV** and stop the culprit drug.`]},{title:`Red flags, referral and follow-up`,points:[`**Reciprocal ST depression** is the best sign that ST elevation is a true STEMI.`,`Posterior MI, de Winter T waves and Sgarbossa-positive LBBB are **STEMI equivalents**.`,`ST elevation in aVR with depression in 6+ leads suggests **left main disease**.`,`**Wellens**: deep or biphasic T inversion in V2-V3 when pain-free; never stress-test.`,`Broad-complex tachycardia is **VT until proved otherwise**; never give verapamil.`,`Pre-excited AF: no adenosine, verapamil, diltiazem, digoxin or beta-blockers.`,`Refer same day: STEMI, Wellens, complete block, Mobitz II, QTc over 500 ms.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Emergency drugs used with the ECG`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Aspirin`,`150-300 mg chewed`,`Then 75-100 mg daily`,`GI bleed`],[`Clopidogrel`,`300 mg with lysis (75 mg if over 75)`,`Then 75 mg daily`,`Bleeding`],[`Calcium gluconate 10%`,`30 mL IV over 10 minutes`,`Repeat if ECG changes persist`,`Protects heart; does not lower potassium`],[`Insulin with glucose`,`10 units soluble insulin with 25 g glucose IV`,`Single dose, repeat as needed`,`Hypoglycaemia; monitor glucose`],[`Salbutamol`,`10-20 mg nebulised`,`Single dose`,`Tremor, tachycardia`],[`Adenosine`,`6 mg rapid IV, then 12 mg, then 18 mg`,`Seconds`,`Flushing, chest discomfort; avoid severe asthma`],[`Amiodarone`,`300 mg IV over 10-20 min, then 900 mg over 24 h`,`24 hours`,`Hypotension; QT prolongation`],[`Atropine`,`500 microgram IV every 3-5 min`,`Up to 3 mg`,`Not in heart transplant`],[`Magnesium sulphate`,`2 g IV over 10 minutes`,`Single dose`,`Flushing; keep potassium 4.5-5.0`]]},{heading:`STEMI localisation`,columns:[`Territory`,`Leads with ST elevation`,`Culprit artery`],rows:[[`Anteroseptal`,`V1-V4`,`LAD`],[`Lateral`,`I, aVL, V5-V6`,`Circumflex or diagonal`],[`Inferior`,`II, III, aVF`,`Right coronary (about 80%)`],[`Right ventricle`,`V4R 1 mm or more`,`Proximal right coronary`],[`Posterior`,`ST depression V1-V3; elevation V7-V9`,`Circumflex or right coronary`]]},{heading:`AV block grading`,columns:[`Grade`,`ECG feature`,`Action`],rows:[[`First degree`,`PR over 0.20 s, every P conducted`,`Usually benign; review drugs`],[`Mobitz I`,`PR lengthens until a beat drops`,`Nodal; benign in young`],[`Mobitz II`,`Constant PR, sudden dropped beat`,`Infranodal; pacemaker`],[`Complete`,`P and QRS independent`,`Pace`]]},{heading:`Hyperkalaemia on the ECG`,columns:[`Potassium (mmol/L)`,`ECG change`],rows:[[`About 5.5-6.5`,`Peaked narrow T waves`],[`6.5-7.5`,`Flat or absent P, long PR`],[`Over 7.5-8`,`Wide QRS, sine wave, VF or asystole`]]}],remember:[`Same order every time: rate, rhythm, axis, intervals, P, QRS, ST, T, compare.`,`Reciprocal change confirms STEMI.`,`Inferior STEMI: V4R before nitrates.`,`Normal ECG never excludes ACS.`,`Broad-complex tachycardia = VT until proved otherwise.`,`PE: sinus tachycardia is commonest; S1Q3T3 is uncommon.`,`Hyperkalaemia: give calcium before the potassium result.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`ERC 2025: peri-arrest doses re-cited (atropine 500 microgram to 3 mg, amiodarone 300 mg over 10-20 min, procainamide 10-15 mg/kg); AF cardioversion first shock 200 J or more (AHA 2025).`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - ECG interpretation`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Recording of cardiac depolarisation/repolarisation from **6 limb (frontal) + 6 chest (horizontal) leads**; standard **25 mm/s, 10 mm/mV** - small square 0.04 s, large 0.20 s.`],[`Relevant classification`,`Rate/rhythm disorders, conduction blocks (AV, BBB, fascicular), chamber enlargement, ischaemia/infarction, metabolic/drug/channelopathy patterns.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`SA node (If) -> atria (P) -> **AV node delay (PR)** -> His-Purkinje (narrow QRS) -> plateau (ST isoelectric) -> repolarisation (T); subendocardial ischaemia -> ST depression; transmural injury -> ST elevation + reciprocal change.`],[`Key history (when to record)`,`**Within 10 minutes** for chest pain, breathlessness, syncope, palpitations; baseline in HT, DM, CKD; suspected hyperkalaemia; overdose; before QT-prolonging drugs; note pain at time of recording.`],[`Key examination (recording technique)`,`V1-V2 **4th ICS** (too high mimics septal MI/Brugada), V4 5th ICS MCL, V5-V6 same level; **V3R-V4R in inferior STEMI**, **V7-V9 when V1-V3 depressed**; arm reversal inverts lead I.`],[`Investigations - systematic reading`,`Identity/calibration -> **rate (300/large squares; irregular x6)** -> rhythm -> **axis (I and aVF, then II)** -> PR -> QRS -> QTc -> P -> QRS voltage/Q -> ST -> T/U -> compare -> one-line conclusion.`],[`Diagnostic criteria - normal values`,`Rate 60-100, **PR 0.12-0.20 s**, **QRS under 0.12 s**, **QTc under 450 (M)/460 (F) ms**, axis -30 to +90, transition V3-V4.`],[`Diagnostic criteria - ischaemia`,`STEMI: **1 mm in 2 contiguous leads; V2-V3 2 mm men 40+, 2.5 mm under 40, 1.5 mm women**; NSTE-ACS: ST depression 0.5 mm or new T inversion; pathological Q over 0.04 s or 25% of R.`],[`Diagnostic criteria - STEMI equivalents`,`**Posterior MI** (ST depression V1-V3, tall R; V7-V9 0.5 mm), **Sgarbossa** in LBBB/paced, **de Winter T**, hyperacute T, **aVR elevation + depression in 6 leads** (left main), **Wellens** (critical LAD - no stress test).`],[`Diagnostic criteria - chamber and conduction`,`LVH: **Sokolow-Lyon 35 mm**, Cornell 28/20 mm; RVH: R V1 7 mm; RBBB rSR' V1; LBBB broad S V1, notched R V6; LAFB axis -45 to -90; **Mobitz II and CHB need pacing**.`],[`Diagnostic criteria - metabolic and drug`,`**Hyperkalaemia**: peaked T -> flat P/long PR -> wide QRS -> sine wave; hypokalaemia U waves; hypocalcaemia long QT; digoxin reverse tick; TCA QRS over 0.10 s, R aVR over 3 mm; PE sinus tachycardia (S1Q3T3 10-20%).`],[`Treatment - emergency`,`Hyperkalaemic ECG: **calcium gluconate 10% 30 mL IV** then insulin 10 U + glucose 25 g, salbutamol 10-20 mg; torsades: **MgSO4 2 g IV**; unstable tachycardia: **synchronised DC**; bradycardia: **atropine 500 microgram to 3 mg**, pacing.`],[`Treatment - tachycardia`,`Narrow regular: Valsalva -> **adenosine 6-12-18 mg**; **broad complex = VT until proved otherwise** - amiodarone 300 mg over 10-20 min; **never verapamil**; pre-excited AF: no AV nodal blockers - DC or procainamide.`],[`Special groups`,`Children: right axis and T inversion V1-V3 normal; athletes: bradycardia, 1st-degree, Mobitz I, early repolarisation normal; women: lower V2-V3 threshold, longer QTc limit.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`STEMI/equivalent, Wellens, aVR pattern, hyperkalaemic changes, CHB/Mobitz II, broad-complex tachycardia, **QTc over 500 ms**, pre-excited AF, Brugada type 1 with syncope.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Serial ECGs 15-30 min during pain and after lysis (50% ST resolution = success); QTc before/after QT drugs (stop if over 500 or rise over 60 ms); keep baseline tracings.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`A normal ECG never excludes ACS; reciprocal depression confirms STEMI; never treat an artefact; arm reversal keeps V6 normal, dextrocardia loses R progression; always compare with an old tracing.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Chest pain ECG: act-now pathway`,steps:[{label:`12-lead ECG within 10 minutes`,detail:`Note pain at time of recording`},{label:`ST elevation 2 contiguous leads?`,detail:`Check V2-V3 sex/age thresholds`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Reciprocal change, no PR depression - STEMI`,detail:`Reperfusion decision now`,tone:`warn`},{label:`No elevation - look for equivalents`,detail:`Posterior, Sgarbossa, de Winter, aVR + diffuse depression`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Inferior STEMI - record V4R; V1-V3 depression - V7-V9`},{label:`Non-diagnostic - repeat every 15-30 min + hs-troponin`,detail:`Wellens: admit, no stress test`},{label:`Diagnosis made and documented with action`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`branch`,heading:`Tachycardia on the ECG: which arm?`,root:`Tachycardia over 100 - unstable? DC cardioversion first`,arms:[{label:`Narrow regular`,steps:[`AVNRT/AVRT or flutter 2:1`,`Valsalva`,`Adenosine 6 then 12 then 18 mg`]},{label:`Narrow irregular`,steps:[`AF / multifocal AT`,`Rate control, CHA2DS2-VA`,`Cardiovert 200 J or more if unstable`]},{label:`Broad regular`,tone:`warn`,steps:[`VT until proved otherwise`,`Amiodarone 300 mg over 10-20 min`,`Never verapamil`]},{label:`Broad irregular`,tone:`warn`,steps:[`Pre-excited AF or torsades`,`No AV nodal blockers - DC or procainamide`,`Torsades: MgSO4 2 g IV`]}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`ST elevation: STEMI versus its commonest mimics`,columns:[`Feature`,`STEMI`,`Acute pericarditis`,`Early repolarisation`],rows:[[`Distribution`,`One coronary territory`,`Widespread, not territorial`,`Mainly V2-V5 (precordial)`],[`ST shape`,`Convex or straight, may be tombstone`,`Concave (saddle)`,`Concave with notched J point`],[`Reciprocal depression`,`Present - confirms STEMI`,`Only aVR and V1`,`Absent`],[`PR segment`,`Normal`,`PR depression (elevation in aVR)`,`Normal`],[`Evolution`,`Q waves, T inversion over hours`,`T inversion after ST normalises`,`Stable on serial ECGs`]]}]},"cardiovascular-pad-vte":{notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Peripheral arterial disease** is atherosclerotic narrowing of the leg arteries.`,`PAD is defined by a resting **ABI of 0.90 or less**; about half are asymptomatic.`,`PAD is a **coronary equivalent**: patients die of MI and stroke, not limb loss.`,`**Venous thromboembolism** covers deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism.`,`VTE is **provoked**, **unprovoked** or **cancer-associated**, which sets treatment duration.`,`In India PAD is driven by **diabetes and tobacco** and often presents as a diabetic foot.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Fontaine**: I asymptomatic, II claudication, III rest pain, IV ulcer or gangrene.`,`**Chronic limb-threatening ischaemia** is rest pain over 2 weeks, ulcer or gangrene.`,`Acute limb ischaemia is graded I viable, IIa and IIb threatened, III irreversible.`,`ABI over 1.40 means **calcified, incompressible arteries**, not normal.`,`**High-risk PE** has shock or systolic under 90 for 15 minutes.`,`Intermediate-high risk PE has **RV dysfunction plus raised troponin**.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`1. Plaque narrows the leg arteries, most often the superficial femoral artery.`,`2. On walking, flow cannot rise, so lactate causes cramp: **claudication**.`,`3. Pain lies **one level below the block**; calf pain means femoro-popliteal disease.`,`4. VTE follows **Virchow's triad**: stasis, hypercoagulability and endothelial injury.`,`5. Calf clot propagates; **proximal DVT** is the one that embolises.`,`6. In PE the right ventricle dilates and fails, causing hypotension and raised troponin.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`True claudication is cramp at a fixed distance, relieved within minutes by standing.`,`**Neurogenic claudication** is eased by sitting or bending forward, with normal pulses.`,`Rest pain at night, relieved by hanging the leg down, means **limb-threatening ischaemia**.`,`In a young smoker with cold fingers and migratory phlebitis, think **Buerger disease**.`,`For a swollen leg ask about surgery, plaster, travel, pregnancy, the pill and past VTE.`,`Ask for cancer clues in unprovoked VTE over 40.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`Look for shiny hairless skin, thick nails, ulcers on toes and heels, and gangrene.`,`Palpate every pulse; the level where pulses vanish maps the block.`,`**Buerger test**: pallor on raising the leg and rubor on hanging it down.`,`Measure the **ABI**: higher ankle pressure divided by higher arm pressure.`,`Acute limb ischaemia: the **6 Ps**; sensory loss and weakness mean immediate threat.`,`DVT: calf 3 cm larger, pitting oedema, tenderness; **Homans sign is unreliable**.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`**ABI** is the first PAD test; use toe-brachial index if ABI is over 1.40.`,`Arterial imaging only when revascularisation is planned.`,`For DVT, score first with the **two-level Wells score**.`,`DVT unlikely: **D-dimer**; if positive, proximal compression ultrasound.`,`DVT likely: ultrasound first; repeat at 6-8 days if negative with raised D-dimer.`,`D-dimer only **rules out**; use an age-adjusted cut-off over 50 years.`,`PE likely (Wells over 4): **CT pulmonary angiography**; V/Q scan in pregnancy.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Complete tobacco cessation** halves amputation and death in PAD.`,`**Structured walking**: 30-60 minutes at least 3 times a week for 12 weeks.`,`Every PAD patient gets a **high-intensity statin**, target LDL under 55 mg/dL.`,`Give a single antiplatelet: **clopidogrel 75 mg** or aspirin 75-150 mg.`,`VTE: start anticoagulation on suspicion if imaging will be delayed.`,`**Apixaban** or **rivaroxaban** need no heparin lead-in.`,`Mobilise early after DVT; bed rest is not needed.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Add **cilostazol 100 mg twice daily** for claudication; stop if no gain at 3 months.`,`Rivaroxaban 2.5 mg twice daily plus aspirin for high-risk PAD with low bleeding risk.`,`Refer for revascularisation if claudication limits life after 3 months of treatment.`,`VTE duration: **3 months** if provoked by a major transient factor.`,`Unprovoked VTE: at least 3 months, then consider indefinite.`,`**Indefinite** for recurrent VTE, antiphospholipid syndrome and active cancer.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`Pregnancy: **weight-based LMWH** for the rest of pregnancy and 6 weeks postpartum.`,`**Antiphospholipid syndrome**: warfarin to INR 2-3; avoid DOACs.`,`Cancer-associated VTE: a factor Xa DOAC or LMWH for at least 6 months.`,`Medical inpatients with **Padua score 4 or more**: enoxaparin 40 mg SC daily.`,`Diabetic foot: daily inspection and **never walk barefoot**.`]},{title:`Red flags, referral and follow-up`,points:[`Acute limb ischaemia: **IV heparin** and vascular referral within the hour.`,`Keep the ischaemic limb dependent; no heat, no elevation, no compression.`,`High-risk PE: **alteplase 100 mg IV over 2 hours**.`,`Intermediate-high PE: anticoagulate and lyse only if the patient deteriorates.`,`A massively swollen blue leg is **phlegmasia cerulea dolens**; refer at once.`,`VTE: review at **3 months** to decide whether to stop or extend.`,`Persisting breathlessness 3-6 months after PE needs echo for CTEPH.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs for PAD and VTE`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Clopidogrel`,`75 mg oral once daily`,`Lifelong in symptomatic PAD`,`Bleeding, dyspepsia`],[`Aspirin`,`75-150 mg oral once daily`,`Lifelong`,`GI upset, bleeding`],[`Atorvastatin`,`40-80 mg oral once daily`,`Lifelong`,`Myalgia, raised ALT`],[`Cilostazol`,`100 mg twice daily, before or after meals`,`Stop if no gain at 3 months`,`Headache, palpitations; never in heart failure`],[`Apixaban (VTE)`,`10 mg twice daily 7 days, then 5 mg twice daily`,`3 months or longer`,`Bleeding; no heparin lead-in`],[`Rivaroxaban (VTE)`,`15 mg twice daily 21 days, then 20 mg daily`,`3 months or longer`,`Bleeding; take with food`],[`Enoxaparin`,`1 mg/kg SC twice daily; prophylaxis 40 mg daily`,`Pregnancy, cancer, lead-in`,`Bleeding, HIT; once daily if CrCl under 30`],[`Warfarin`,`5 mg daily with 5 days LMWH overlap`,`INR 2-3; APS long term`,`Bleeding, teratogenic, interactions`],[`Alteplase`,`100 mg IV over 2 hours`,`Once, high-risk PE`,`Major and intracranial bleeding`]]},{heading:`ABI and Fontaine at a glance`,columns:[`Finding`,`Meaning`,`Action`],rows:[[`ABI 1.00-1.40`,`Normal`,`Reassure`],[`ABI 0.90 or less`,`PAD`,`Statin, antiplatelet, tobacco stop, walking`],[`ABI 0.40 or less`,`Severe PAD`,`Urgent vascular referral`],[`ABI over 1.40`,`Calcified arteries`,`Toe-brachial index`],[`Fontaine III-IV`,`Rest pain, ulcer, gangrene`,`Urgent revascularisation`]]},{heading:`Two-level Wells score for DVT`,columns:[`Item`,`Points`],rows:[[`Active cancer`,`1`],[`Leg paralysis or recent plaster`,`1`],[`Bedridden 3 days or surgery within 12 weeks`,`1`],[`Tenderness along deep veins`,`1`],[`Whole leg swollen; or calf 3 cm larger`,`1 each`],[`Pitting oedema; collateral superficial veins`,`1 each`],[`Previous DVT`,`1`],[`Alternative diagnosis as likely`,`-2`]]}],remember:[`PAD is a coronary equivalent: statin, antiplatelet, stop tobacco.`,`ABI over 1.40 is abnormal: use toe pressure.`,`Structured walking beats every drug; cilostazol never in heart failure.`,`Wells first, D-dimer only if unlikely; D-dimer never rules in.`,`Apixaban and rivaroxaban need no heparin lead-in; dabigatran and edoxaban do.`,`3 months provoked, indefinite for cancer, APS and recurrence.`,`LMWH in pregnancy, warfarin in APS, lysis only for high-risk PE.`,`Acute limb ischaemia: 6 Ps, heparin, surgeon within the hour.`]},checked:`2026-10`,updated:[`AHA/ACC 2026 PE guideline: massive/submassive replaced by clinical categories A-E; systemic lysis not routine in A-C, reasonable in E1, may be considered in D.`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - PAD and venous thromboembolism`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**PAD**: atherosclerotic limb artery stenosis, **ABI 0.90 or less**; **VTE**: DVT (proximal or distal) and PE - provoked, unprovoked or cancer-associated.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Fontaine I-IV** (IIa over 200 m, IIb under 200 m); CLTI = rest pain over 2 weeks/ulcer/gangrene; acute limb ischaemia Rutherford I-III; **PE: ESC high / intermediate-high / intermediate-low / low risk; AHA/ACC 2026 categories A-E**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`PAD: **tobacco (all forms), diabetes**, HT, lipids, CKD, Buerger in young smokers; VTE: **Virchow triad** - surgery, immobility, cancer, pregnancy, oestrogen, thrombophilia, APS.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`PAD: fixed stenosis -> exercise supply-demand mismatch -> **claudication one level below block**; VTE: calf valve-pocket clot -> proximal extension -> embolism -> **RV pressure overload -> shock**.`],[`Key history`,`Claudication distance and relief on standing (vs neurogenic - sitting); rest pain relieved by dependency; ulcers; ED/buttock pain; swollen leg - surgery, travel, OCP, pregnancy, cancer clues, previous VTE.`],[`Key examination`,`Trophic changes, all pulses, bruits, **Buerger angle**, capillary refill, monofilament; **measure ABI**; 6 Ps in acute ischaemia; DVT - calf 3 cm larger, pitting, tenderness; PE - RR, SpO2, HR, BP.`],[`Investigations`,`ABI (over 1.40 - **toe-brachial index under 0.70**), lipids, HbA1c, duplex only if revascularising; **Wells DVT 2 or more** - ultrasound, under 2 - D-dimer (age x 10 over 50); PE: Wells over 4 - CTPA; V/Q in pregnancy/CKD.`],[`Treatment - first line (PAD)`,`**Stop tobacco**, **structured walking 30-60 min 3x/week for 12 weeks**, **high-intensity statin LDL under 55**, **clopidogrel 75 mg** (or aspirin), BP 120-129, foot care; cilostazol 100 mg BD if needed (never in HF).`],[`Treatment - escalation (PAD)`,`Rivaroxaban 2.5 mg BD + aspirin (COMPASS/VOYAGER) in high ischaemic, low bleeding risk; DAPT only 1-6 months after endovascular therapy; **revascularise if lifestyle-limiting at 3 months or CLTI**.`],[`Treatment - first line (VTE)`,`**Apixaban 10 mg BD x 7 days then 5 mg BD** or **rivaroxaban 15 mg BD x 21 days then 20 mg OD**; dabigatran/edoxaban after 5 days LMWH; enoxaparin 1 mg/kg BD; warfarin INR 2-3 with 5-day overlap.`],[`Treatment - duration (VTE)`,`**3 months provoked**; unprovoked - 3 months then consider indefinite; **indefinite for recurrence, APS, active cancer**; extended apixaban 2.5 BD / rivaroxaban 10 mg OD.`],[`Emergencies`,`Acute limb ischaemia: **UFH 5000 U IV**, limb dependent, vascular surgeon within the hour (6-h window); high-risk PE (category E): **alteplase 100 mg over 2 h**, noradrenaline, fluids under 500 mL.`],[`Special groups`,`Pregnancy: **LMWH** to 6 weeks postpartum (min 3 months); cancer: apixaban/rivaroxaban/edoxaban or LMWH 6 months+; **APS: warfarin, not DOAC**; diabetic foot: toe pressure; Buerger: only tobacco cessation works.`],[`Complications`,`PAD: CLTI, amputation, MI/stroke (main cause of death); VTE: PE, recurrence, **post-thrombotic syndrome 20-50%**, **CTEPH 2-4%**, bleeding, HIT.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`6 Ps; rest pain/gangrene; **phlegmasia cerulea dolens**; PE with SBP under 90 or syncope; ABI over 1.40 with ulcer; unprovoked VTE with cancer clues.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`PAD 3 months then 6-12 monthly (walking distance, foot, ABI, LDL, tobacco); VTE review at 3 months to stop/extend, yearly bleeding/renal check; dyspnoea 3-6 months after PE - echo for CTEPH.`],[`Prevention / programme`,`Medical inpatients Padua 4 or more - **enoxaparin 40 mg OD**; post hip/knee replacement LMWH 28-35 days; early mobilisation; travel advice; stop oestrogen after VTE.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**AHA/ACC 2026 PE**: categories A-E (A incidental, B low score - early discharge, C raised score/RV strain, D normotensive shock, E hypotension); lysis/catheter therapy for E, consider D.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Claudicants die of MI; ABI over 1.40 is abnormal; D-dimer only rules out; apixaban/rivaroxaban need no heparin lead-in; stockings do not prevent PTS; bed rest not needed in DVT.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Suspected PE: score, test, categorise`,steps:[{label:`Breathless, pleuritic pain, tachycardia, risk factor`},{label:`Shock or SBP under 90?`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Yes - bedside echo, UFH, lysis (category E)`,detail:`Alteplase 100 mg over 2 h`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Stable - Wells PE score`,detail:`PERC if very low risk`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Over 4 - CTPA; 4 or less - D-dimer first`,detail:`Age-adjusted cut-off over 50; V/Q in pregnancy`},{label:`Confirmed - sPESI/Hestia, troponin, RV`,detail:`AHA/ACC category B vs C-D`},{label:`Low risk (sPESI 0) - home DOAC`,detail:`Apixaban 10 mg BD 7 days then 5 mg BD`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`PAD: from claudication to limb salvage`,steps:[{label:`All: stop tobacco, statin, antiplatelet`,detail:`Atorvastatin 40-80 mg LDL under 55; clopidogrel 75 mg`},{label:`Structured walking 12 weeks`,detail:`30-60 min, 3x/week, walk to moderate pain`},{label:`Add cilostazol 100 mg BD`,detail:`Before meals; stop at 3 months if no gain; never in HF`,tone:`warn`},{label:`High-risk: rivaroxaban 2.5 mg BD + aspirin`,detail:`Low bleeding risk only`},{label:`Lifestyle-limiting at 3 months or CLTI - revascularise`,detail:`Duplex then CTA/MRA; endovascular or bypass`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Leg pain on walking: three look-alikes`,columns:[`Feature`,`Arterial claudication`,`Neurogenic claudication`,`Venous claudication`],rows:[[`Onset`,`Fixed walking distance`,`On standing or walking, variable`,`Bursting pain with walking`],[`Relief`,`Standing still within minutes`,`Sitting or bending forward`,`Leg elevation`],[`Pulses`,`Reduced, ABI 0.90 or less`,`Normal`,`Normal; swollen leg`],[`Uphill/cycling`,`Worse uphill`,`Easier uphill, cycling tolerated`,`Variable`]]}]}};export{e as default};